24c28582 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 134.4KB 137.95s
24c28582 [deepseek-v4-flash] #No explicit question was attached, so here are a few review notes from reading through both files.
24c28582 [deepseek-v4-flash] #I didn’t run them; these are static observations.
24c28582 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c28582 [deepseek-v4-flash] #• assembler.ts*
24c28583 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c28583 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- It’s a parser/label tracker so far, not yet a code generator. `ram` is allocated but never
24c28583 [deepseek-v4-flash] #written to, and the assembled bytes column is hard-coded to `00 00 00`.
24c28584 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Any line that doesn’t start with a label is misparsed. `ORG 8000` and `LDA #00` will treat the
24c28584 [deepseek-v4-flash] #first word as a label and then read the opcode from the wrong position.
24c28584 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Operand length decisions use truthiness: `00` and `0000` are treated as “no operand”, which will
24c28584 [deepseek-v4-flash] #produce wrong instruction sizes.
24c28585 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Forward label references are all treated as 3-byte absolute references, but branch targets should
24c28585 [deepseek-v4-flash] #be 2-byte relative offsets. There is no separate mode for relative branches.
24c28585 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Only lowercase `,x` and `),y` are handled. Uppercase suffixes like `,X` and `),Y` fall through to
24c28585 [deepseek-v4-flash] #label handling.
24c28586 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `($nn,X)` indirect addressing is not implemented.
24c28586 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `($nnnn),Y` is incorrectly treated as absolute-Y; on real 6502 this mode uses a zero-page
24c28586 [deepseek-v4-flash] #pointer.
24c28586 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c28587 [deepseek-v4-flash] #• machine.ts*
24c28587 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c28587 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- JSR/RTS appears to be off by one. JSR pushes `pc+2` and RTS adds another 1, so the return address
24c28588 [deepseek-v4-flash] #becomes `pc+3` instead of `pc+2`. On 6502, JSR pushes `return-1`, and RTS adds 1 after pulling.
24c28588 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `machineRom` is set to `plus4`, but `isC64` is true. Plus/4 hardware uses TED rather than
24c28588 [deepseek-v4-flash] #VIC/CIA, so the C64 I/O handling may not match the selected ROM unless this is intentional.
24c28588 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The interpreter is a large handwritten switch. Many valid opcodes aren’t implemented yet, so any
24c28589 [deepseek-v4-flash] #program that hits one will stop with “unsupported opcode”.
24c28589 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The static opcode table contains `mode` fields but the interpreter mostly ignores them. Decoding
24c28589 [deepseek-v4-flash] #via that table would reduce the risk of switch-case drift.
24c28589 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- C64 color RAM at `$D800` is written to memory, but the page-based switch in `writeAddress`
24c2858a [deepseek-v4-flash] #doesn’t handle it specially and logs it as unknown.
24c2858a [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `readCIA` just returns raw memory, so CIA timers/keyboard input aren’t really emulated yet.
24c2858a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c2858b [deepseek-v4-flash] #Overall the direction is clear, but both files look like work-in-progress scaffolding rather than
24c2858b [deepseek-v4-flash] #complete implementations. Let me know if you’d like me to focus on a specific fix or refactor.
24c2858b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
