24c420e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 32.57KB 12.86s
24c420e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Yes — a physical disk track is a continuous ring of bits, and the `.nib` track of 6,656 bytes
24c420e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #stores that entire ring as a fixed-size circular buffer.
24c420ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c420ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #So:
24c420ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c420ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Byte 0 and byte 6,655 are physically adjacent on the disk.
24c420eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #- When the drive reads a sector, it doesn't start at a fixed "track beginning"; it starts wherever
24c420eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #the read head happens to be. The nibble stream just wraps around.
24c420eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Your nibble reader should use modulo indexing:
24c420ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c420ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #  const nibble = trackNibbles[(offset + position) % 6656];
24c420ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c420ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #  or wrap manually when `position` reaches 6656.
24c420ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c420ed [deepseek-v4-flash] #The `6656` count itself is 16 sectors × 416 raw nibbles per sector — that's the full formatted
24c420ed [deepseek-v4-flash] #ring, so it lines up nicely.
24c420ed [deepseek-v4-flash] #
