24c4244b [deepseek-v4-flash] #Yes, exactly. Because the track is circular, you can't just read from byte 0. You need to scan
24c4244c [deepseek-v4-flash] #for a start marker — the address prologue `D5 AA 96` (and then the data prologue `D5 AA AD`) —
24c4244c [deepseek-v4-flash] #and then decode the bytes that follow.
24c4244c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c4244c [deepseek-v4-flash] #Here's the typical approach:
24c4244d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c4244d [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Search the 6656-byte track for `D5 AA 96`.
24c4244d [deepseek-v4-flash] #   When found, the next 3 bytes form the address field (track, sector, volume, checksum).
24c4244e [deepseek-v4-flash] #   That tells you which sector you're at.
24c4244e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c4244e [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Then search for `D5 AA AD` (the data prologue).
24c4244e [deepseek-v4-flash] #   After that, the next 342 nibbles are the 6-and-2 encoded data for that sector.
24c4244f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c4244f [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. Decode those 342 nibbles with your existing `decodeDos33Sector()` (which already does
24c4244f [deepseek-v4-flash] #the 6-and-2 → 256-byte decompression).
24c4244f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c42450 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. Because it's circular, when you read past byte 6655 you wrap to byte 0.
24c42450 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   You can either:
24c42450 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   - use `% 6656` indexing, or
24c42450 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   - copy the track twice into a 13312-byte buffer and search there (many emulators do this).
24c42451 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c42451 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So yes — detect start, then decode. That's the standard DOS 3.3 nibble-reading flow.
24c42451 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
