24c55da1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c55da1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. Minor issues
24c55da1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c55da1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `async function loadNib(...): Array<Track>` should be `Promise<Array<Track>>`.
24c55da2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `decodeDos33Sector` uses a shared global `temp`. Make it local to avoid stale data on errors.
24c55da2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The unused `Nibbles` interface and `testBraille` import can be removed.
24c55da2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `payload` as a global is unnecessary; declare it inside `loadNib` and return tracks.
24c55da2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c55da3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Once the address offsets are fixed, you should see proper track/sector values in the logs, and
24c55da3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #you’ll be one step away from mounting the decoded disk in your Disk II emulation.
24c55da3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56504 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 62.35KB 14.09s
24c56504 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Good progress — the address field offsets are now correct (`volume, track, sector, crc`). That
24c56504 [deepseek-v4-flash] #should give you proper values in the log.
24c56505 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56505 [deepseek-v4-flash] #A few remaining issues to be aware of:
24c56505 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56506 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Sector count is now 32 per track, not 16.
24c56506 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   You push `i` for address prologues and `-i` for data prologues, so `sectors.length` counts both.
24c56506 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you want the real sector count, count only non-negative entries:
24c56506 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56507 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56507 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   const addressCount = sectors.filter(v => v >= 0).length;
24c56507 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56507 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56508 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   or just keep separate arrays.
24c56508 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56508 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Decoded sectors are not linked to their track/sector numbers.
24c56508 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   You push the raw decoded 256 bytes into `blocks`, but you don't know which track/sector they
24c56509 [deepseek-v4-flash] #belong to. The data prologue alone doesn't tell you; you need to match it with the preceding
24c56509 [deepseek-v4-flash] #address prologue (the closest `D5 AA 96` before it).
24c56509 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c56509 [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. The checksum nibble is not read.
24c5650a [deepseek-v4-flash] #   After the 342 data nibbles, DOS 3.3 stores a checksum value (at `dataStart + 342`) and then `DE
24c5650a [deepseek-v4-flash] #AA EB`. You don't have to verify it for loading, but if you plan to write sectors later you'll need
24c5650a [deepseek-v4-flash] #to generate it.
24c5650a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c5650b [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. The global `temp` array is reused across calls.
24c5650b [deepseek-v4-flash] #   That's fine for single-threaded use, but be careful: if `decodeDos33Sector` fails partway, stale
24c5650b [deepseek-v4-flash] #data from the previous sector remains in `temp[0..k-1]`. Local allocation is safer.
24c5650b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c5650c [deepseek-v4-flash] #Next step: build a `decodeTrack` that returns `{ track, sector, bytes }` for each sector, then
24c5650c [deepseek-v4-flash] #store them into a `disk[track][sector]` array. Once you have that, you can mount the result in your
24c5650c [deepseek-v4-flash] #Disk II emulation and actually boot the game. Want help wiring that up?
24c5650c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
