24c57a90 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 68.64KB 10.78s
24c57a90 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`pass` is mostly false because you're checking for the wrong epilogue.
24c57a90 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a90 [deepseek-v4-flash] #After the 342 data nibbles and the checksum byte, a DOS 3.3 data field ends with:
24c57a91 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a91 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a91 [deepseek-v4-flash] #DE AA EB
24c57a91 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a92 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a92 [deepseek-v4-flash] #not `D5 AA 96`. `D5 AA 96` is the address prologue that begins the next sector, and
24c57a92 [deepseek-v4-flash] #usually there are several gap bytes between the end of one field and the start of the next. So
24c57a92 [deepseek-v4-flash] #checking `bytes[dataStart + 343..345]` for `D5 AA 96` will almost always fail.
24c57a93 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a93 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Correct check:
24c57a93 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a93 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a94 [deepseek-v4-flash] #const epilogue1 = bytes[(dataStart + 343) % n];
24c57a94 [deepseek-v4-flash] #const epilogue2 = bytes[(dataStart + 344) % n];
24c57a94 [deepseek-v4-flash] #const epilogue3 = bytes[(dataStart + 345) % n];
24c57a94 [deepseek-v4-flash] #const pass = epilogue1 === 0xDE && epilogue2 === 0xAA && epilogue3 === 0xEB;
24c57a95 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a95 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a95 [deepseek-v4-flash] #You can also verify the checksum itself: it should equal the XOR of all 342 decoded 6-bit values
24c57a95 [deepseek-v4-flash] #(i.e., `temp[0..341]` before merging into 256 bytes). That’s a stronger test than the epilogue.
24c57a96 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a96 [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you want to see `pass` true consistently, use the epilogue check above. If you still see
24c57a96 [deepseek-v4-flash] #failures, the track may be copy-protected or the sector layout is non-standard.
24c57a96 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57a97 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The bigger task is still linking each decoded block to its `track`/`sector`, and building a
24c57a97 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`disk[track][sector]` array. Once that’s done, you can mount it in the Disk II emulation.
24c57a97 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57cfe [nitro@ryzen5] thanks!
24c57db0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 74.58KB 7.81s
24c57db0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Good — with `DE AA EB` as the epilogue, `pass` should now be true for every data field on a
24c57db0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #standard DOS 3.3 track.
24c57db0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57db1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The remaining piece is that `detectSectors` collects decoded blocks but never links them to their
24c57db1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`track`/`sector` numbers. Right now the address fields are logged, and the data fields are decoded,
24c57db1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #but the two aren't paired. To build a usable disk image you'll want to:
24c57db1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57db2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Scan for an address prologue, read `track`/`sector`.
24c57db2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Find the next data prologue after it (search forward up to ~600 bytes, wrapping).
24c57db2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. Decode those 342 nibbles into 256 bytes.
24c57db2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. Store into `disk[track][sector]`.
24c57db3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57db3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #That's the key integration step for mounting the disk in your Disk II emulation.
24c57db3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57db3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Also, if you want to be stricter, verify the checksum after decoding — it's just XOR of all 342
24c57db4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #decoded 6-bit values before the 6-and-2 merge. But for loading, it's optional.
24c57db4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57db4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #One more small thing: `sectors` mixes positive (address offsets) and negative (data offsets), so
24c57db4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the count is 32 per track. If you ever want to display "16 sectors", filter for `>= 0`.
24c57db5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24c57db5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Nice progress — you're one step away from booting `RESCUERAIDERS.nib` in the emulator.
24c57db5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
