24e9452a [nitro@ryzen5] when the vic20 flashed its cursor what underlying effect does it use?
24e945c1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 3.605KB 8.98s
24e945c1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The VIC‑20’s cursor is generated by the VIC chip (6560/6561) directly. It’s not a character
24e945c1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #that the CPU changes; it’s a separate overlay.
24e945c2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24e945c2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The VIC chip has a cursor enable bit that, when set, displays a solid block at the
24e945c2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #current screen position.
24e945c2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The chip automatically toggles that bit on/off every frame (or every other frame
24e945c3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #depending on the chip revision), producing the blink — the CPU does nothing.
24e945c3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The KERNAL ROM only manages where the cursor is (the screen pointer) and turns it on/off with
24e945c3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #routines like `CHROUT` or `CURS_ON`/`CURS_OFF`.
24e945c3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
24e945c4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So the effect is a hardware‑generated, blinking overlay block, not a software‑drawn
24e945c4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #character inversion or color change. That’s why the cursor blinks smoothly without any CPU
24e945c4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #overhead.
24e945c4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
