24e9496e [claude-haiku-4-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 4.986KB 2.74s
24e9496e [claude-haiku-4-5] #Neither — it's hardware blinking in the VIC chip itself.
24e9496e [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e9496f [claude-haiku-4-5] #The VIC-20's cursor is generated directly by the VIC chip (6560/6561), not by changing the screen
24e9496f [claude-haiku-4-5] #RAM or character ROM. When cursor is enabled, the chip:
24e9496f [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e9496f [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Draws a solid block at the cursor position each frame
24e94970 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Toggles visibility automatically (on/off rhythm) without CPU intervention
24e94970 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Uses the chip's internal timing, independent of any character data
24e94970 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94970 [claude-haiku-4-5] #So it's pure hardware overlay — the VIC chip just flips a signal to show/hide that block
24e94971 [claude-haiku-4-5] #repeatedly. No background color tricks, no character swaps. The CPU only tells it where to
24e94971 [claude-haiku-4-5] #draw and whether to enable it.
24e94971 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94971 [claude-haiku-4-5] #That's the elegant part: the blink is "free" — zero CPU cycles wasted on it.
24e94972 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94bd8 [nitro@ryzen5] how does keyboard sensing work
24e94c1c [claude-haiku-4-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 5.952KB 3.58s
24e94c1c [claude-haiku-4-5] #The VIC-20's keyboard is a 6x7 matrix scanned by the CIA1 chip (6526).
24e94c1c [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94c1c [claude-haiku-4-5] #• How it works:*
24e94c1c [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94c1d [claude-haiku-4-5] #1. Matrix layout: Keys are arranged in rows and columns. The CIA1 outputs to one row at a
24e94c1d [claude-haiku-4-5] #time (via Port A), and reads back which columns have a key pressed (via Port B).
24e94c1d [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94c1e [claude-haiku-4-5] #2. Software polling: The KERNAL ROM repeatedly:
24e94c1e [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Sets one row line low (active low)
24e94c1e [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Reads the column lines
24e94c1e [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - If a column reads low, that key is pressed
24e94c1f [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Moves to the next row, repeats
24e94c1f [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94c1f [claude-haiku-4-5] #3. Debouncing: The ROM waits a bit between reads to filter out bouncing contacts.
24e94c1f [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94c1f [claude-haiku-4-5] #4. Interrupt option: CIA1 can also generate an interrupt when any key is pressed
24e94c20 [claude-haiku-4-5] #(flag bit), so the CPU doesn't have to poll constantly — but early VIC-20 code often just polls
24e94c20 [claude-haiku-4-5] #anyway.
24e94c20 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24e94c20 [claude-haiku-4-5] #• Result*: The CPU discovers which key is down by testing each row/column combination until
24e94c21 [claude-haiku-4-5] #it finds a low signal. It's simple matrix scanning with no dedicated keyboard controller — the CIA1
24e94c21 [claude-haiku-4-5] #just provides the I/O lines.
24e94c21 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
