24ea146c [claude-haiku-4-5] #Looking at your machine.ts - solid 6502 emulator foundation. A few observations:
24ea146d [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea146d [claude-haiku-4-5] #• Strong points:*
24ea146d [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Clean instruction dispatch, good coverage of addressing modes
24ea146d [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Proper flag handling (carry, overflow, zero, negative)
24ea146e [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Page boundary cycle penalties implemented
24ea146e [claude-haiku-4-5] #- VIC-20, C64, Plus4, Apple II support threaded through
24ea146e [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea146e [claude-haiku-4-5] #• Issues I'd flag:*
24ea146f [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea146f [claude-haiku-4-5] #1. `readAddress2` vs `readAddress` - You have both. `readAddress2` does the actual work,
24ea146f [claude-haiku-4-5] #`readAddress` wraps it just to set `memOp`. This is fine but consider if that side effect is always
24ea1470 [claude-haiku-4-5] #desirable.
24ea1470 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1470 [claude-haiku-4-5] #2. ADC/SBC decimal mode - Logic looks correct but untested on actual decimal operations.
24ea1470 [claude-haiku-4-5] #The nibble arithmetic is right, but verify against real hardware edge cases (half-carry flag
24ea1470 [claude-haiku-4-5] #nuances).
24ea1470 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1471 [claude-haiku-4-5] #3. Indirect JMP bug (6502 quirk) - You got it:
24ea1471 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1471 [claude-haiku-4-5] #const highAddress=(pointer & 0xFF00) | ((pointer + 1) & 0x00FF);
24ea1472 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1472 [claude-haiku-4-5] #This is correct - wraps within page. Good catch.
24ea1472 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1472 [claude-haiku-4-5] #4. RTI status register restoration:
24ea1473 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1473 [claude-haiku-4-5] #st=(st & ~0x10) | 0x20; // clear B, set unused bit
24ea1473 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1473 [claude-haiku-4-5] #You're clearing B flag on RTI, which is correct (B is only set on hardware interrupts via BRK). The
24ea1474 [claude-haiku-4-5] #unused bit 5 stays 1. Solid.
24ea1474 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1474 [claude-haiku-4-5] #5. Branch penalty cycles - You add 1 on branch taken, and another if page crossed. Correct.
24ea1474 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1475 [claude-haiku-4-5] #• Questions:*
24ea1475 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1475 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Are you testing the VIC-20 keyboard matrix scan? The notes show the layout but I don't see CIA1
24ea1476 [claude-haiku-4-5] #port A/B reads being properly masked for keyboard input.
24ea1476 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- The `tickVic()` method sets raster but doesn't actually drive the VIC chip state machine. Is that
24ea1476 [claude-haiku-4-5] #intentional (just display, no video timing)?
24ea1476 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- In `displayAppleWide`, you're checking `(this.input[0]&0x40)==0` for blanking. Is `input[0]` bit
24ea1476 [claude-haiku-4-5] #6 really your blanking flag, or should it be checking raster timing directly?
24ea1477 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24ea1477 [claude-haiku-4-5] #The code is substantial and well-structured. The assembler and NIB loader are useful companions.
24ea1477 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
