24eb6df7 [claude-haiku-4-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 128.8KB 5.88s
24eb6df7 [claude-haiku-4-5] #On real C64, the cursor is not a VIC chip hardware feature—it's entirely
24eb6df8 [claude-haiku-4-5] #software-managed.
24eb6df8 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6df8 [claude-haiku-4-5] #• How it actually works:*
24eb6df8 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6df9 [claude-haiku-4-5] #1. Character replacement: The cursor is displayed by writing a specific character code to
24eb6df9 [claude-haiku-4-5] #screen RAM at the cursor position
24eb6df9 [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Typically A0 (reverse video space) or 7F (underscore-like glyph)
24eb6df9 [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Or a custom character from the character ROM
24eb6dfa [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6dfa [claude-haiku-4-5] #2. Blinking via software:
24eb6dfa [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - The BASIC interpreter (or application) polls a timer and periodically writes/erases the cursor
24eb6dfa [claude-haiku-4-5] #character
24eb6dfb [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Uses a counter in zero-page RAM (typically around A2-A4 area in Commodore BASIC)
24eb6dfb [claude-haiku-4-5] #   - Every N frames, it XORs or toggles the character at cursor position
24eb6dfb [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6dfb [claude-haiku-4-5] #3. No VIC chip involvement: The VIC-II doesn't track cursor state—it just renders whatever
24eb6dfc [claude-haiku-4-5] #character code is in screen RAM at that location
24eb6dfc [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6dfc [claude-haiku-4-5] #• In your emulator context:*
24eb6dfc [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6dfd [claude-haiku-4-5] #Your current approach is closer to correct—you'd need to:
24eb6dfd [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Store cursor position (row/column)
24eb6dfd [claude-haiku-4-5] #- During `displayC64()` render, check if current char position matches cursor
24eb6dfd [claude-haiku-4-5] #- If `frameCount & 32` (or similar blink rate), substitute/invert that character
24eb6dfe [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6dfe [claude-haiku-4-5] #The cursor isn't an overlay; it's a character replacement in the displayed text, just like typing
24eb6dfe [claude-haiku-4-5] #normally overwrites screen RAM.
24eb6dfe [claude-haiku-4-5] #
24eb6dff [claude-haiku-4-5] #Would you want to add cursor rendering to the display functions?
24eb6dff [claude-haiku-4-5] #
