25190e8e [claude-haiku-4-5] #FFFX Addresses - 6502 Interrupt Vectors
25190e8e [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e8f [claude-haiku-4-5] #The $FFFx region contains the 6502 processor's interrupt vector table at the top of memory:
25190e8f [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e8f [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e8f [claude-haiku-4-5] #$FFFA-$FFFB  NMI vector    (Non-Maskable Interrupt)
25190e90 [claude-haiku-4-5] #$FFFC-$FFFD  RESET vector  (Power-on / Reset)
25190e90 [claude-haiku-4-5] #$FFFE-$FFFF  IRQ vector    (Interrupt Request)
25190e90 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e91 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e91 [claude-haiku-4-5] #Each is a 16-bit little-endian address (low byte first).
25190e91 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e91 [claude-haiku-4-5] #In machine.ts
25190e92 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e92 [claude-haiku-4-5] #You're setting these vectors during `reset()`:
25190e92 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e93 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e93 [claude-haiku-4-5] #this.memory[0xFFFA] = nmi & 0xff;      // NMI low
25190e93 [claude-haiku-4-5] #this.memory[0xFFFB] = nmi >> 8;        // NMI high
25190e94 [claude-haiku-4-5] #this.memory[0xFFFC] = reset & 0xff;    // RESET low
25190e94 [claude-haiku-4-5] #this.memory[0xFFFD] = reset >> 8;      // RESET high
25190e94 [claude-haiku-4-5] #this.memory[0xFFFE] = irq & 0xff;      // IRQ low
25190e94 [claude-haiku-4-5] #this.memory[0xFFFF] = irq >> 8;        // IRQ high
25190e95 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e95 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e95 [claude-haiku-4-5] #In notes.txt
25190e95 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e96 [claude-haiku-4-5] #The trace shows TED reading/writing these addresses during initialization—the Plus/4 ROM is
25190e96 [claude-haiku-4-5] #configuring its interrupt handlers at startup. The pattern suggests the ROM is copying or verifying
25190e96 [claude-haiku-4-5] #handler addresses into RAM before enabling interrupts.
25190e96 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
25190e97 [claude-haiku-4-5] #The values shown (`FFA4`, `F2F6`, etc.) are the actual handler entry points in the Plus/4 ROM.
25190e97 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
