253cba6c [claude-haiku-4-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.003 33.02KB 7.85s
253cba6c [claude-haiku-4-5] #Good—now I can see the full instruction set definition. The `addressmode` array and `profile` specs
253cba6c [claude-haiku-4-5] #are key to understanding the encoding.
253cba6c [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cba6c [claude-haiku-4-5] #• Mapping specs to addressing modes:*
253cba6d [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cba6d [claude-haiku-4-5] #Looking at the `profile` array indices (0-15) and the instruction specs, I can correlate them:
253cba6d [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cba6d [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 0: 1 byte, 2 cycles → Implied (CLC, NOP, etc.)
253cba6e [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 1: 1 byte, 3 cycles → Stack (PHA, PHP)
253cba6e [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 2: 1 byte, 4 cycles → Stack (PLA, PLP)
253cba6e [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 3: 1 byte, 6 cycles → Stack (RTI, RTS)
253cba6e [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 4: 1 byte, 7 cycles → BRK only
253cba6f [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 5: 2 bytes, 2 cycles → Branch (BCC, BEQ, etc.)
253cba6f [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 6: 3 bytes, 6 cycles → JSR (absolute)
253cba6f [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 7: 2-3 bytes → BIT instruction variants
253cba6f [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 8: 3 bytes, 3-5 cycles → JMP (absolute or indirect)
253cba70 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 9: 2-3 bytes → CPX/CPY (immediate, zeropage, absolute)
253cba70 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 10: 2-3 bytes → STX/STY
253cba70 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 11: 2-3 bytes, 5-7 cycles → DEC (zeropage variants to absolute)
253cba70 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 12: 1-3 bytes → ASL, LSR, ROL, ROR (accumulator + addressing modes)
253cba71 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 13: 2-3 bytes → LDX/LDY variants
253cba71 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 14: 2-3 bytes → STA (zeropage to indirect,Y)
253cba71 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- spec 15: 2-3 bytes → ADC, AND, CMP, EOR, LDA, ORA, SBC (most flexible: immediate through
253cba71 [claude-haiku-4-5] #indirect,Y)
253cba72 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cba72 [claude-haiku-4-5] #• For AND specifically* (spec 15):
253cba72 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Opcodes: `29 25 35 2D 3D 39 21 31`
253cba72 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- Maps to: immediate, zeropage, zeropage,X, absolute, absolute,X, absolute,Y, (indirect,X),
253cba73 [claude-haiku-4-5] #(indirect),Y
253cba73 [claude-haiku-4-5] #- `29` = immediate mode
253cba73 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cba73 [claude-haiku-4-5] #The `assembler.ts` needs to map opcode bytes back to addressing modes. Currently it's detecting the
253cba74 [claude-haiku-4-5] #mode from syntax but not selecting the correct opcode byte based on that mode.
253cba74 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cba74 [claude-haiku-4-5] #Would you like me to show how to restructure the opcode selection logic to use both the detected
253cba75 [claude-haiku-4-5] #addressing mode and the opcode list from the JSON spec?
253cba75 [claude-haiku-4-5] #
253cbc05 [nitro@ryzen5] yes
