257933e8 [claude-sonnet-5] #Yes — `<p>` in HTML5 has what's called an "optional end tag." You don't need `</p>` at all; the
257933e8 [claude-sonnet-5] #parser automatically closes it when it hits certain other block-level elements (another `<p>`, a
257933e8 [claude-sonnet-5] #heading, a `<div>`, a `<ul>`, etc.). That's exactly what's happening in this file already — the
257933e8 [claude-sonnet-5] #`<p>` before "raw payload..." has no closing tag and it's perfectly valid HTML.
257933e9 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257933e9 [claude-sonnet-5] #This is different from XHTML, where every element must be explicitly closed (`<p>...</p>`
257933e9 [claude-sonnet-5] #or self-closed). Since this doc has no XML declaration or `xhtml` doctype, you're free to leave
257933e9 [claude-sonnet-5] #`<p>` unclosed as you're already doing.
