257953be [claude-sonnet-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.018 43.66KB 14.94s
257953be [claude-sonnet-5] #That happens because Chrome runs its language-detection heuristic on any text content it renders in
257953be [claude-sonnet-5] #the tab — including raw JSON viewed directly in the browser — and decides whether to offer
257953bf [claude-sonnet-5] #translation based on what language it thinks the bulk of the visible text is in.
257953bf [claude-sonnet-5] #
257953bf [claude-sonnet-5] #Given what's in these files — Cherokee syllabary, Coptic, Greek, Hebrew, Vai, Ogham, hieroglyph
257953bf [claude-sonnet-5] #codepoints, fullwidth Latin — it's not surprising Chrome's detector gets confused. If a JSON file
257953c0 [claude-sonnet-5] #has a lot of non-Latin script values packed together, the detector can decide the "page language"
257953c0 [claude-sonnet-5] #is something other than English and pop the translate banner, even though structurally it's just
257953c0 [claude-sonnet-5] #JSON key/value data, not prose in another language.
257953c0 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257953c1 [claude-sonnet-5] #A few things that influence this:
257953c1 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257953c1 [claude-sonnet-5] #- No `lang` attribute or `Content-Language` header — when serving JSON directly (not
257953c1 [claude-sonnet-5] #wrapped in HTML), there's nothing telling Chrome "this is English content with embedded non-English
257953c2 [claude-sonnet-5] #strings." It falls back to sniffing the raw text.
257953c2 [claude-sonnet-5] #- Ratio of script matters — a file like `machinespec.json` (mostly English prose in values)
257953c2 [claude-sonnet-5] #is less likely to trigger it than something like the Cherokee or Vai syllabary lists, where huge
257953c2 [claude-sonnet-5] #blocks of non-Latin characters dominate the sample Chrome scans.
257953c3 [claude-sonnet-5] #- This is purely a browser-side UX thing — it doesn't affect the actual data or how
257953c3 [claude-sonnet-5] #`bibli.js` parses/uses the JSON. It's Chrome being "helpful" when you view the raw file directly in
257953c3 [claude-sonnet-5] #a tab.
257953c3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257953c4 [claude-sonnet-5] #If it bothers you and you want to suppress it specifically for the raw JSON views, the usual fix is
257953c4 [claude-sonnet-5] #either serving the file with an explicit `Content-Language: en` header, or (if wrapping it in an
257953c4 [claude-sonnet-5] #HTML view instead of raw JSON) adding `<html lang="en">`. Since your actual data display goes
257953c4 [claude-sonnet-5] #through `index.html` and `bibli.js` rather than users opening the raw `.json` directly, this is
257953c5 [claude-sonnet-5] #likely only something you notice yourself when spot-checking files in the browser — not something
257953c5 [claude-sonnet-5] #end users viewing the rendered page would hit.
257953c5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
