25795af2 [claude-sonnet-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.027 48.21KB 19.24s
25795af2 [claude-sonnet-5] #There's no native CommonMark/GFM syntax for this — markdown itself has no `lang` attribute
25795af3 [claude-sonnet-5] #mechanism. But a few conventions exist depending on what you're trying to solve:
25795af3 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af3 [claude-sonnet-5] #• 1. YAML frontmatter (whole-document language)*
25795af3 [claude-sonnet-5] #Common in Jekyll/Hugo/most static site generators:
25795af4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af4 [claude-sonnet-5] #---
25795af4 [claude-sonnet-5] #lang: en
25795af4 [claude-sonnet-5] #---
25795af5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af5 [claude-sonnet-5] #This tags the document as a whole, which doesn't really help for a file that's mostly
25795af5 [claude-sonnet-5] #Unicode codepoint tables rather than prose in one language.
25795af5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af6 [claude-sonnet-5] #• 2. Inline HTML passthrough (per-block/per-span)*
25795af6 [claude-sonnet-5] #Since markdown allows raw HTML to pass through untouched, you can wrap specific content:
25795af6 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af6 [claude-sonnet-5] #<span lang="am">ሀለሐመሠረሰሸቀ</span>
25795af7 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af7 [claude-sonnet-5] #or for a whole section:
25795af7 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af7 [claude-sonnet-5] #<div lang="am">
25795af8 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af8 [claude-sonnet-5] ## U+1300x	ሀ	ለ	ሐ	መ	ሠ	ረ	ሰ	ሸ
25795af8 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af8 [claude-sonnet-5] #</div>
25795af9 [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795af9 [claude-sonnet-5] #This is honestly the most correct fix for your Ethiopic block case, since `lang` on a wrapping
25795af9 [claude-sonnet-5] #element is exactly what screen readers and browser rendering/font-fallback logic look at.
25795afa [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795afa [claude-sonnet-5] #• 3. Pandoc-style span/div attributes*
25795afa [claude-sonnet-5] #If you're using Pandoc as your markdown processor, there's an extended syntax:
25795afa [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795afb [claude-sonnet-5] #[ሀለሐመ]{lang="am"}
25795afb [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795afb [claude-sonnet-5] #but this is Pandoc-specific, not portable to GitHub's renderer or most other parsers.
25795afb [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795afc [claude-sonnet-5] #• Worth noting for your case specifically: a codepoint chart isn't really "written in"
25795afc [claude-sonnet-5] #a language — it's data, not prose. Tagging `lang="am"` on an Ethiopic glyph table is a bit of a
25795afc [claude-sonnet-5] #category mismatch (there's no Amharic sentence being spoken). The `lang` attribute's real
25795afc [claude-sonnet-5] #value here is more pragmatic than semantic: it hints to the renderer "use a font/fallback chain
25795afd [claude-sonnet-5] #that can render this script,"* which is exactly the tofu-box problem you ran into. So even if it's
25795afd [claude-sonnet-5] #not linguistically perfect, wrapping unicode block tables in `<div lang="am">` (or whatever's
25795afd [claude-sonnet-5] #closest) is likely to actually fix the rendering rather than just being decorative.
25795afd [claude-sonnet-5] #
25795afe [claude-sonnet-5] #If GitHub Pages is the target renderer, worth testing whether it respects `lang` on inline
25795afe [claude-sonnet-5] #`<div>`/`<span>` for font fallback — GitHub's own CSS does pull in fairly broad web font coverage
25795afe [claude-sonnet-5] #already, so the `??` boxes might be a local-editor-only problem rather than something that needs
25795afe [claude-sonnet-5] #fixing in the markdown itself.
25795aff [claude-sonnet-5] #
