257961cf [claude-sonnet-5] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.027 50.60KB 19.21s
257961cf [claude-sonnet-5] #"Lore" in the markdown-convention sense is a fun rabbit hole — there's a fair bit of
257961cf [claude-sonnet-5] #folklore/tribal-knowledge around markdown that never made it into any spec but persists because
257961d0 [claude-sonnet-5] #tools or communities adopted it. A few threads worth knowing about:
257961d0 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d0 [claude-sonnet-5] #• The "flavors" schism*
257961d0 [claude-sonnet-5] #Markdown was never really one thing after John Gruber's original 2004 spec — it was famously
257961d1 [claude-sonnet-5] #underspecified, which led to a decade of incompatible implementations (PHP Markdown Extra,
257961d1 [claude-sonnet-5] #Maruku, Redcarpet, Discount...) all disagreeing on edge cases like nested lists or emphasis inside
257961d1 [claude-sonnet-5] #words. This is the actual reason CommonMark exists — an attempt in 2014 to write a formal,
257961d1 [claude-sonnet-5] #unambiguous grammar so two parsers would agree on the same input. It's telling that even now,
257961d2 [claude-sonnet-5] #"markdown" without qualification is meaningless without knowing whose markdown.
257961d2 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d2 [claude-sonnet-5] #• GFM's quiet feature creep*
257961d3 [claude-sonnet-5] #GitHub Flavored Markdown added a bunch of things over the years that leaked into general assumption
257961d3 [claude-sonnet-5] #despite never being part of core markdown: task lists (`- [ ] todo`), tables via pipes,
257961d3 [claude-sonnet-5] #`~~strikethrough~~`, automatic URL linking, and `@mentions`/`#issue` autolinking (GitHub-specific,
257961d3 [claude-sonnet-5] #invisible elsewhere). A lot of people write "markdown" today that's really unrenderable outside
257961d3 [claude-sonnet-5] #GitHub's own pipeline.
257961d4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d4 [claude-sonnet-5] #• The "setext" headers nobody uses*
257961d4 [claude-sonnet-5] #Original markdown supports underlining text with `===` or `---` to make it an H1/H2:
257961d4 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d5 [claude-sonnet-5] #Title
257961d5 [claude-sonnet-5] #=====
257961d5 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d5 [claude-sonnet-5] #Almost nobody does this anymore in favor of `# Title`, but it's still valid everywhere and
257961d6 [claude-sonnet-5] #occasionally you'll see it in older READMEs like a fossil.
257961d6 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d6 [claude-sonnet-5] #• Reference-style links — the forgotten feature*
257961d6 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d6 [claude-sonnet-5] #[text][1]
257961d7 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d7 [claude-sonnet-5] #[1]: https://example.com
257961d7 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d8 [claude-sonnet-5] #Designed for cleaner plain-text readability (so the URL doesn't clutter the prose), barely used now
257961d8 [claude-sonnet-5] #that most editors auto-format inline links, but it's a genuinely nice convention for documents with
257961d8 [claude-sonnet-5] #heavy citation/link density — which, actually, might suit a project like yours with lots of
257961d8 [claude-sonnet-5] #external references.
257961d8 [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961d9 [claude-sonnet-5] #• The lone backslash escape mess*
257961d9 [claude-sonnet-5] #Every implementation has slightly different rules about which punctuation needs escaping (`\*`,
257961d9 [claude-sonnet-5] #`\_`, `\[`) and it's one of the most common cross-renderer breakage points — something that looks
257961d9 [claude-sonnet-5] #fine on GitHub can render wrong in VS Code preview or Obsidian.
257961da [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961da [claude-sonnet-5] #• Emoji shortcodes as folklore, not spec*
257961da [claude-sonnet-5] #`:tada:` → 🎉 is pure GitHub/Slack convention, not markdown at all — it's a separate
257961da [claude-sonnet-5] #text-replacement pass bolted on by specific platforms. If you're already doing raw Unicode emoji
257961db [claude-sonnet-5] #directly in your files, you're bypassing this shortcode-lore entirely, which is arguably the
257961db [claude-sonnet-5] #"purer" way of doing it.
257961db [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961db [claude-sonnet-5] #• Admonition blocks — the multi-tool disagreement*
257961dc [claude-sonnet-5] #Things like:
257961dc [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961dc [claude-sonnet-5] #> **Note:** some text
257961dc [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961dc [claude-sonnet-5] #vs. Obsidian/Docusaurus-style:
257961dd [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961dd [claude-sonnet-5] #> [!NOTE]
257961dd [claude-sonnet-5] #> some text
257961dd [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961de [claude-sonnet-5] #are a great example of convention without standard — everyone invented their own "callout
257961de [claude-sonnet-5] #box" syntax and none of them agree, so what renders as a fancy colored box in Obsidian is just a
257961de [claude-sonnet-5] #plain blockquote with literal `[!NOTE]` text on GitHub.
257961de [claude-sonnet-5] #
257961df [claude-sonnet-5] #Given your project's flavor — lots of raw Unicode, multilingual scripts, bibliographic/spec
257961df [claude-sonnet-5] #cataloguing — the CommonMark vs GFM tension and the reference-link convention are probably the two
257961df [claude-sonnet-5] #most practically relevant bits of lore for you. Is there a particular corner of this you
257961e0 [claude-sonnet-5] #want to go deeper on — like whether GitHub Pages' renderer (Jekyll + kramdown, typically) has its
257961e0 [claude-sonnet-5] #own quirks worth knowing?
257961e0 [claude-sonnet-5] #
