257c4674 [nitro@ryzen5] the key stream encoding in terminals is an ansi standard?
257c4a11 [kimi-k3] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.032 513B 57.33s
257c4a12 [kimi-k3] #Partly. The framing is standardized, but the actual key encodings are mostly de facto conventions.
257c4a12 [kimi-k3] #
257c4a12 [kimi-k3] #• What is standardized:* The structure of the byte sequences comes from ECMA-48 (ISO/IEC
257c4a12 [kimi-k3] #6429), which is essentially identical to the withdrawn ANSI X3.64-1979 — that's where the term
257c4a13 [kimi-k3] #"ANSI escape codes" comes from. It defines the grammar: the ESC introducer, CSI (`ESC [`), SS3
257c4a13 [kimi-k3] #(`ESC O`), parameter bytes, and final bytes. Separately, the plain control characters (Ctrl+A =
257c4a13 [kimi-k3] #0x01, Tab = 0x09, Esc = 0x1B) come from ASCII/ISO 646, which is why Tab and Ctrl+I are
257c4a13 [kimi-k3] #indistinguishable, as are Enter and Ctrl+M.
257c4a14 [kimi-k3] #
257c4a14 [kimi-k3] #• What isn't: ECMA-48 mainly covers control functions sent to the terminal.
257c4a14 [kimi-k3] #What a terminal sends when you press* a key was defined by whoever made the device, and DEC's
257c4a14 [kimi-k3] #choices became the baseline everyone copies:
257c4a15 [kimi-k3] #
257c4a15 [kimi-k3] #- Arrow keys → `CSI A/B/C/D` (or `SS3` in application mode) — VT100
257c4a15 [kimi-k3] #- Home/End/Insert/Delete/PgUp/PgDn → `CSI H/F/2~/3~/5~/6~` — VT220-ish
257c4a15 [kimi-k3] #- F5+ → `CSI 1x~` — VT220
257c4a16 [kimi-k3] #- Modifier parameters like `CSI 1;5A` for Ctrl+Up — xterm's invention, widely imitated, not in any
257c4a16 [kimi-k3] #formal standard
257c4a16 [kimi-k3] #
257c4a16 [kimi-k3] #There's real divergence too: Backspace sending BS vs DEL, rxvt's different Home/End encodings,
257c4a17 [kimi-k3] #application-mode variants. This is exactly why terminfo/termcap exists — a database to paper over
257c4a17 [kimi-k3] #the fact that there's no single agreed encoding.
257c4a17 [kimi-k3] #
257c4a17 [kimi-k3] #Modern terminals are still patching the gaps. The kitty keyboard protocol and the fixterms proposal
257c4a18 [kimi-k3] #exist specifically because the legacy encoding is ambiguous (you can't tell ESC alone from Alt+key,
257c4a18 [kimi-k3] #Ctrl+arrows needed bolt-on extensions, etc.).
257c4a18 [kimi-k3] #
257c4a18 [kimi-k3] #So: "ANSI" is the colloquial name, ECMA-48 is the actual standard for the sequence format, and the
257c4a19 [kimi-k3] #key mappings themselves are DEC/xterm folklore.
257c4a19 [kimi-k3] #
