25ba04e8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 7.813KB 29.98s
25ba04e8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #From the JSON you supplied:
25ba04e8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba04e8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Instruction mnemonics (specs): 67
25ba04e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Addressing modes: 10
25ba04e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba04e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #That list of mnemonics includes many that expand to multiple opcodes; for example, `LD`, `JP`,
25ba04e9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`CALL`, and `ADD` each cover many operand/addressing-mode combinations. The JSON itself does not
25ba04ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #contain per-mnemonic opcode counts, so the actual “unique opcode” tally cannot be derived from it
25ba04ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #alone.
25ba04ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba04ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #If you need Z80 opcode-count figures, those come from the full opcode maps (primary, CB, ED, DD, FD
25ba04ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #pages). Typical quoted figures are:
25ba04ea [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba04eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #- 158 documented instruction types (mnemonics)
25ba04eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #- 252 documented opcode variants when all addressing modes are counted
25ba04eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #- 10 addressing modes (as Zilog defines them)
25ba04eb [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba04ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #But that depends on how you count undocumented opcodes and prefix combinations.
25ba04ec [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba08de [nitro@ryzen5] how about we use a 10 bit mask to identify which addressing modes an instruction supports?
25ba09a4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 8.910KB 12.02s
25ba09a4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Yes, a 10‑bit mask is an excellent way to compactly encode which addressing modes an instruction
25ba09a4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #can use. It makes programmatic checks trivial — for example, `(mask & (1 << BIT_INDEXED)) != 0`
25ba09a4 [deepseek-v4-flash] #tells you whether an indexed variant exists.
25ba09a5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09a5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Here's how you could map your existing `addressmode` array to fixed bit positions:
25ba09a5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09a5 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09a6 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 0: immediate
25ba09a6 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 1: immediate extended
25ba09a6 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 2: modified page zero
25ba09a7 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 3: relative
25ba09a7 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 4: extended
25ba09a7 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 5: indexed
25ba09a7 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 6: register
25ba09a8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 7: register indirect
25ba09a8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 8: implied
25ba09a8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bit 9: bit address
25ba09a8 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Then each instruction object in your JSON could include a field like `"addrModesMask"`:
25ba09a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09a9 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #"LD": {
25ba09aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #  "addrModesMask": 0b01101111
25ba09aa [deepseek-v4-flash] #}
25ba09ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #Or, for readability, you could list the modes as an array and compute the mask at load time:
25ba09ab [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #"LD": {
25ba09ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #  "addressModes": [
25ba09ac [deepseek-v4-flash] #    "immediate",
25ba09ad [deepseek-v4-flash] #    "immediate extended",
25ba09ad [deepseek-v4-flash] #    "extended",
25ba09ad [deepseek-v4-flash] #    "indexed",
25ba09ad [deepseek-v4-flash] #    "register",
25ba09ae [deepseek-v4-flash] #    "register indirect"
25ba09ae [deepseek-v4-flash] #  ]
25ba09ae [deepseek-v4-flash] #}
25ba09af [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09af [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09af [deepseek-v4-flash] #Both work; the mask is just a more compact serialisation.
25ba09af [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09b0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #• Caveats to keep in mind:*
25ba09b0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09b0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- The mask describes all addressing modes used by any variant of the instruction. A single
25ba09b0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #variant like `LD (HL), n` actually uses two modes at once (register indirect and immediate), but
25ba09b1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the mask still captures both.
25ba09b1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- If you later decide to document individual opcodes or variants, you'll need a separate mask per
25ba09b1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #variant. The mnemonic‑level mask is a convenient summary, not a full opcode map.
25ba09b1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Because there are exactly 10 modes, a 10‑bit mask fits neatly into a `uint16`, leaving six unused
25ba09b2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #bits for future extensions.
25ba09b2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba09b2 [deepseek-v4-flash] #This approach is clean, human‑readable, and machine‑efficient. I'd say go for it.
25ba09b3 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
