25ba224d [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 14.78KB 21.62s
25ba224d [deepseek-v4-flash] #No — not always. In the Z80, opcodes for instructions that don't encode a register are not
25ba224e [deepseek-v4-flash] #assigned in a single, strictly sequential list. The opcode map is organized by functional groups,
25ba224e [deepseek-v4-flash] #and individual opcodes may be:
25ba224e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba224e [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Sequentially placed within a block – e.g., RST: 0x00, 0x08, 0x10, … 0x38 (step 8).
25ba224e [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Scattered across unrelated instructions – e.g., NOP = 0x00, HALT = 0x76, DI = 0xF3, EI =
25ba224f [deepseek-v4-flash] #0xFB.
25ba224f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Grouped by condition bits – e.g., conditional calls: 0xC4, 0xCC, 0xD4, 0xDC, … (the
25ba224f [deepseek-v4-flash] #condition field orders them in a predictable pattern, but they're not a single run).
25ba224f [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Placed under a prefix – e.g., ADC HL,BC = 0xED 0x4A, ADC HL,DE = 0xED 0x5A, ADC HL,HL =
25ba2250 [deepseek-v4-flash] #0xED 0x6A, ADC HL,SP = 0xED 0x7A. Those are sequential in the high nibble of the second
25ba2250 [deepseek-v4-flash] #byte, but only within the ED-prefixed block.
25ba2250 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2251 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So if you're building a spec, you can't derive the actual opcode value from the mnemonic + mode
25ba2251 [deepseek-v4-flash] #mask alone. The mask tells you which addressing modes exist, but the exact opcode (and even
25ba2251 [deepseek-v4-flash] #the number of opcodes) depends on the Z80's internal encoding scheme — which mixes register fields,
25ba2251 [deepseek-v4-flash] #condition fields, and fixed bit patterns.
25ba2252 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba23d4 [nitro@ryzen5] the question is for those that encode a register, and those that encode a register pair
25ba2530 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 16.21KB 21.30s
25ba2531 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Let me answer both parts:
25ba2531 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2531 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Instructions with register encoding
25ba2531 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2532 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Yes — they follow a fixed, regular pattern in the opcode map. The register (or register
25ba2532 [deepseek-v4-flash] #pair) is encoded directly into specific bits of the opcode, so the resulting opcodes are not
25ba2532 [deepseek-v4-flash] #sequential as `+1` steps but are arithmetic progressions with a constant step.
25ba2533 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2533 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Single registers (8‑bit)
25ba2533 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The register code is a 3‑bit field, usually `r = 000,001,010,011,100,101,110,111` for
25ba2533 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`B,C,D,E,H,L,(HL),A`.
25ba2534 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Example: `LD r, n` has format `00 r 110`. The opcodes are:
25ba2534 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2534 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2534 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD B,n  0x06   (r=000)
25ba2535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD C,n  0x0E   (r=001)   +8
25ba2535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD D,n  0x16   (r=010)   +8
25ba2535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD E,n  0x1E   (r=011)   +8
25ba2535 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD H,n  0x26   (r=100)   +8
25ba2536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD L,n  0x2E   (r=101)   +8
25ba2536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD (HL),n 0x36 (r=110)   +8
25ba2536 [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD A,n  0x3E   (r=111)   +8
25ba2537 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2537 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2537 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So they are sequential in steps of 8.
25ba2537 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The exception is the `(HL)` entry, but it still fits the pattern.
25ba2538 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2538 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Similarly, `LD r, r'` (`0x40 + r*8 + r'`) gives 64 opcodes in a single contiguous block
25ba2538 [deepseek-v4-flash] #`0x40–0x7F`, ordered by the `r` and `r'` fields.
25ba2538 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2539 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Register pairs (16‑bit)
25ba2539 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Most pair‑based instructions use a 2‑bit field `rp`:
25ba2539 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2539 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- For `LD rr,nn`, `ADD HL,rr`, `ADC HL,rr`, etc., the mapping is:
25ba253a [deepseek-v4-flash] #  `00 = BC`, `01 = DE`, `10 = HL`, `11 = SP`.
25ba253a [deepseek-v4-flash] #  Format: `00 rp0 001`, so opcodes are:
25ba253a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253b [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD BC,nn  0x01
25ba253b [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD DE,nn  0x11   +0x10
25ba253b [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD HL,nn  0x21   +0x10
25ba253c [deepseek-v4-flash] #LD SP,nn  0x31   +0x10
25ba253c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253c [deepseek-v4-flash] #A clean step of `0x10`.
25ba253c [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253d [deepseek-v4-flash] #- For `PUSH`/`POP`, the mapping is `00 = BC`, `01 = DE`, `10 = HL`, `11 = AF`.
25ba253d [deepseek-v4-flash] #  Format: `11 rp0 101`, giving `0xC5, 0xD5, 0xE5, 0xF5`.
25ba253d [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253d [deepseek-v4-flash] #- Conditional jumps/calls/returns use a 3‑bit condition field that also follows a regular order
25ba253e [deepseek-v4-flash] #(`000 = NZ, 001 = Z, 010 = NC, 011 = C, 100 = PO, 101 = PE, 110 = P, 111 = M`), yielding opcodes
25ba253e [deepseek-v4-flash] #spaced by 8.
25ba253e [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba253f [deepseek-v4-flash] #• So for any instruction family that carries a register or condition field, the opcodes are
25ba253f [deepseek-v4-flash] #assigned in a predictable, increasing order according to that field.* That’s why assemblers can
25ba253f [deepseek-v4-flash] #often compute the opcode from the operands.
25ba253f [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2540 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Instructions without register encoding
25ba2540 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2540 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Here the opcodes are not systematic — they are chosen individually, often with no
25ba2540 [deepseek-v4-flash] #arithmetic relationship. Examples:
25ba2541 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2541 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2541 [deepseek-v4-flash] #NOP       0x00
25ba2541 [deepseek-v4-flash] #HALT      0x76
25ba2542 [deepseek-v4-flash] #DI        0xF3
25ba2542 [deepseek-v4-flash] #EI        0xFB
25ba2542 [deepseek-v4-flash] #CPL       0x2F
25ba2543 [deepseek-v4-flash] #SCF       0x37
25ba2543 [deepseek-v4-flash] #CCF       0x3F
25ba2543 [deepseek-v4-flash] #DAA       0x27
25ba2543 [deepseek-v4-flash] #RLA       0x17
25ba2544 [deepseek-v4-flash] #RRCA      0x0F
25ba2544 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2544 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2544 [deepseek-v4-flash] #These do not occupy a sequential block. Even within a related group, e.g. rotate instructions:
25ba2545 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2545 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2545 [deepseek-v4-flash] #RLCA  0x07
25ba2545 [deepseek-v4-flash] #RLA   0x17
25ba2546 [deepseek-v4-flash] #RRCA  0x0F
25ba2546 [deepseek-v4-flash] #RRA   0x1F
25ba2546 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2547 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2547 [deepseek-v4-flash] #They are scattered, though they share a bit‑pattern (`00 xxx 111` with a 3‑bit "operation" field).
25ba2547 [deepseek-v4-flash] #But the point is: you cannot infer the opcode from a simple sequence.
25ba2547 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2548 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Summary
25ba2548 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba2548 [deepseek-v4-flash] #╭─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
25ba2548 [deepseek-v4-flash] #│  Encoding               │  Opcode assignment                                         │
25ba2549 [deepseek-v4-flash] #├─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
25ba2549 [deepseek-v4-flash] #│  Register (3‑bit)       │  Always a fixed step (typically +8) per register code      │
25ba2549 [deepseek-v4-flash] #│  Register pair (2‑bit)  │  Always a fixed step (+0x10) per pair code                 │
25ba2549 [deepseek-v4-flash] #│  Condition (3‑bit)      │  Always a fixed step (+8) per condition code               │
25ba254a [deepseek-v4-flash] #│  No register/condition  │  Arbitrary, no sequence; based on functional bit grouping  │
25ba254a [deepseek-v4-flash] #╰─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
25ba254a [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25ba254b [deepseek-v4-flash] #So if you are building a spec or a disassembler: for instructions that encode a register or
25ba254b [deepseek-v4-flash] #condition, you can rely on the arithmetic progression. For those that don’t, you need explicit
25ba254b [deepseek-v4-flash] #opcode tables.
25ba254b [deepseek-v4-flash] #
