25e23ad6 [nitro@ryzen5] addressing modes of 6800 cpu
25e23c80 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.003 494B 25.14s
25e23c80 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The Motorola 6800 CPU has 6 addressing modes:
25e23c81 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c81 [deepseek-v4-flash] #1. Inherent (Implied)
25e23c81 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   No operand is needed; the instruction implies what to do.
25e23c81 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Examples: `NOP`, `INX`, `DEX`, `CLRA`, `TAB`
25e23c81 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c81 [deepseek-v4-flash] #2. Immediate
25e23c82 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The operand is a constant value in the instruction.
25e23c82 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Indicated with `#`.
25e23c82 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Example: `LDAA #$20`
25e23c82 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c82 [deepseek-v4-flash] #3. Direct
25e23c83 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   An 8-bit address is given; the high byte of the address is assumed to be `$00` (zero page).
25e23c83 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Example: `LDAA 20` means load from address `0020`
25e23c83 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c83 [deepseek-v4-flash] #4. Extended
25e23c84 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   A full 16-bit address is given.
25e23c84 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Example: `LDAA $2000`
25e23c84 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c84 [deepseek-v4-flash] #5. Indexed
25e23c84 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The effective address is formed by adding an 8-bit offset to the X register.
25e23c85 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   The 6800 only has the X index register.
25e23c85 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Example: `LDAA 10,X` loads from address `X + 10`
25e23c85 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c85 [deepseek-v4-flash] #6. Relative
25e23c85 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Used for branch instructions.
25e23c85 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   An 8-bit signed offset is added to the program counter.
25e23c86 [deepseek-v4-flash] #   Example: `BRA label`, `BNE label`
25e23c86 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e23c86 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Some sources also mention an Accumulator mode for instructions like `ASLA`, `COMA`, etc.,
25e23c86 [deepseek-v4-flash] #but those are usually classified as inherent.
25e23c86 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2ac72 [nitro@ryzen5] in 6502 is this a legal opcode?
25e2ac7c [nitro@ryzen5] unsupported opcode 110 ($6E)
25e2acee [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 1.837KB 6.85s
25e2acee [deepseek-v4-flash] #In the 6502, opcode `$6E` (hex) is legal.
25e2acee [deepseek-v4-flash] #It is the official instruction:
25e2acee [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2acef [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `ROR absolute` — Rotate Right memory location (16-bit address).
25e2acef [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2acef [deepseek-v4-flash] #Example: `ROR $1234`
25e2acef [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2acf0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So if an emulator or disassembler reports `$6E` as “unsupported,” that is incorrect for a standard
25e2acf0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #6502.
25e2acf0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2acf0 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Just to avoid confusion:
25e2acf1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- `$6E` = 110 decimal
25e2acf1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- On the 6800, `$6E` is also legal — it is `JMP indexed`.
25e2acf1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- But on the 6502, `$6E` is ROR absolute.
25e2acf1 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e234 [nitro@ryzen5] what does atari dvg halt instruction
25e2e361 [deepseek-v4-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 2.534KB 17.52s
25e2e361 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The HALT instruction is the end-of-display-list marker for the Atari DVG (Digital
25e2e361 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Vector Generator).
25e2e361 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e361 [deepseek-v4-flash] #What it does
25e2e362 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e362 [deepseek-v4-flash] #When the DVG encounters the HALT opcode while reading the vector list from memory, it stops all
25e2e362 [deepseek-v4-flash] #further processing:
25e2e362 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- It stops moving the electron beam.
25e2e363 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- It sets a status/flag (e.g., the “HALT” condition) that the CPU can detect.
25e2e363 [deepseek-v4-flash] #- It does not blank the beam or alter the current position; it simply ceases operation.
25e2e363 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e364 [deepseek-v4-flash] #The CPU can check this flag to know that the current frame’s vector list has been fully executed.
25e2e364 [deepseek-v4-flash] #After updating the vector list for the next frame, the CPU starts the DVG again by writing to the
25e2e364 [deepseek-v4-flash] #“GO” (start) register.
25e2e364 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e365 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Instruction encoding
25e2e365 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e365 [deepseek-v4-flash] #On the Atari DVG (used in games like Asteroids and Lunar Lander), the HALT
25e2e365 [deepseek-v4-flash] #instruction is typically the 16-bit word `0x0000` (all bits zero). In the 4-bit opcode
25e2e366 [deepseek-v4-flash] #field, this corresponds to opcode `$0`.
25e2e366 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e366 [deepseek-v4-flash] #So a typical display list ends with:
25e2e366 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e367 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e367 [deepseek-v4-flash] #...   (drawing commands)
25e2e367 [deepseek-v4-flash] #HALT  ; 0x0000
25e2e367 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e368 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e368 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Why it matters
25e2e368 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
25e2e368 [deepseek-v4-flash] #Without a HALT, the DVG would keep trying to fetch and execute memory past the end of the list,
25e2e369 [deepseek-v4-flash] #producing garbage or possibly crashing the hardware. The HALT provides a clean way to end each
25e2e369 [deepseek-v4-flash] #frame and synchronise with the CPU.
25e2e369 [deepseek-v4-flash] #
