25f7dee4 [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.019 23.47KB 27.92s
25f7dee5 [glm-5.2] #Thank you for sharing your work in progress. The structure of your JSON is clean, and your primary
25f7dee5 [glm-5.2] #opcode map in the `encoding` object is highly accurate. It looks like you have a great foundation
25f7dee5 [glm-5.2] #for a Z80 specification.
25f7dee5 [glm-5.2] #
25f7dee6 [glm-5.2] #I have a few observations and thoughts on the data, particularly regarding the `instruction` object
25f7dee6 [glm-5.2] #and how you might handle the remaining WIP elements:
25f7dee6 [glm-5.2] #
25f7dee6 [glm-5.2] #• 1. The `ADC` Forms Array*
25f7dee6 [glm-5.2] #You have started populating the `forms` array for `ADC`, which is a great approach. I noticed it is
25f7dee7 [glm-5.2] #currently missing a few variants:
25f7dee7 [glm-5.2] #• It includes `88` (ADC A,B) but is missing the rest of the 8-bit register variants (`89` through
25f7dee7 [glm-5.2] #`8F`).
25f7dee8 [glm-5.2] #• It is missing the other 16-bit `ADC HL,rr` variants (`ED 5A` for DE, `ED 6A` for HL, and `ED 7A`
25f7dee8 [glm-5.2] #for SP).
25f7dee8 [glm-5.2] #• Depending on your parser, you might also want to consider how to represent the undocumented `ADC
25f7dee8 [glm-5.2] #A,(IX+d)` style instructions, or if you plan to strictly support only the documented set.
25f7dee9 [glm-5.2] #
25f7dee9 [glm-5.2] #• 2. Prefix Handling (CB, ED, DD, FD)*
25f7dee9 [glm-5.2] #Your `encoding` map correctly identifies the prefix bytes. Since you are mapping `forms` using
25f7dee9 [glm-5.2] #strings like `"ED 4A"`, it implies your engine will look up multi-byte opcodes.
25f7deea [glm-5.2] #• Will you be adding separate encoding maps for the `CB`, `ED`, `DD`, and `FD` prefixed
25f7deea [glm-5.2] #instructions, or will those exclusively live inside the `forms` arrays of the individual
25f7deea [glm-5.2] #instructions?
25f7deea [glm-5.2] #• If you keep them in the `forms` arrays, you might want to define how displacement bytes (the `d`
25f7deeb [glm-5.2] #in `(IX+d)`) are represented in your string format, as they will need to be parsed dynamically.
25f7deeb [glm-5.2] #
25f7deeb [glm-5.2] #• 3. Flag Representation*
25f7deec [glm-5.2] #Using `"SZHPNC"` for the `flags` field in `ADC` is a concise way to show which flags are affected.
25f7deec [glm-5.2] #• One minor detail: in your `flagNames` array, you list `"N"` as just `"N"`. While standard, it is
25f7deec [glm-5.2] #usually referred to as "Add/Subtract" or "Subtract" in Z80 documentation. You might consider
25f7deec [glm-5.2] #updating the label to `"Add/Subtract (N)"` for clarity, especially if this JSON will be used to
25f7deed [glm-5.2] #generate UI or documentation.
25f7deed [glm-5.2] #
25f7deed [glm-5.2] #• 4. Empty Instruction Objects*
25f7deed [glm-5.2] #The remainder of your `instruction` dictionary is empty. When you get around to filling those in,
25f7deed [glm-5.2] #keeping the `{"forms": [...], "label": "...", "flags": "..."}` structure consistent will make your
25f7deee [glm-5.2] #life much easier when writing the emulator or assembler that consumes this data.
25f7deee [glm-5.2] #
25f7deee [glm-5.2] #Overall, this is a very solid start. The layout is logical and easy to read. Let me know if you
25f7deee [glm-5.2] #would like to discuss strategies for structuring the prefixed instructions or any other part of the
25f7deef [glm-5.2] #specification!
25f7deef [glm-5.2] #
