25f7f03c [nitro@ryzen5] for the instruction form, should i consider multidim forms field?
25f7f1a6 [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.020 27.17KB 22.08s
25f7f1a6 [glm-5.2] #Yes, moving to a multidimensional or structured `forms` field would be highly beneficial,
25f7f1a6 [glm-5.2] #especially for an assembler or disassembler.
25f7f1a6 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1a7 [glm-5.2] #A flat array of strings (like `"ED 4A"`) tells you the opcode bytes, but it doesn't tell you what
25f7f1a7 [glm-5.2] #operands the instruction expects, how many bytes the total instruction takes, or where the dynamic
25f7f1a7 [glm-5.2] #values (like `n`, `nn`, or `d`) fit into the byte sequence.
25f7f1a8 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1a8 [glm-5.2] #By making `forms` an array of objects, you can map the opcode directly to its syntax and structure.
25f7f1a8 [glm-5.2] #This makes the JSON self-documenting and much easier to parse programmatically.
25f7f1a8 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1a9 [glm-5.2] #Here is an example of how you might structure it using a multidimensional approach:
25f7f1a9 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1a9 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1a9 [glm-5.2] #"ADC": {
25f7f1aa [glm-5.2] #	"label": "add with carry",
25f7f1aa [glm-5.2] #	"flags": "SZHPNC",
25f7f1aa [glm-5.2] #	"forms": [
25f7f1aa [glm-5.2] #		{
25f7f1ab [glm-5.2] #			"opcodes": ["CE"],
25f7f1ab [glm-5.2] #			"syntax": "ADC A,n",
25f7f1ab [glm-5.2] #			"length": 2,
25f7f1ac [glm-5.2] #			"operands": ["A", "n"]
25f7f1ac [glm-5.2] #		},
25f7f1ac [glm-5.2] #		{
25f7f1ac [glm-5.2] #			"opcodes": ["88"],
25f7f1ad [glm-5.2] #			"syntax": "ADC A,B",
25f7f1ad [glm-5.2] #			"length": 1,
25f7f1ad [glm-5.2] #			"operands": ["A", "B"]
25f7f1ad [glm-5.2] #		},
25f7f1ae [glm-5.2] #		{
25f7f1ae [glm-5.2] #			"opcodes": ["ED", "4A"],
25f7f1ae [glm-5.2] #			"syntax": "ADC HL,BC",
25f7f1ae [glm-5.2] #			"length": 2,
25f7f1ae [glm-5.2] #			"operands": ["HL", "BC"]
25f7f1af [glm-5.2] #		},
25f7f1af [glm-5.2] #		{
25f7f1af [glm-5.2] #			"opcodes": ["DD", "8E"],
25f7f1b0 [glm-5.2] #			"syntax": "ADC A,(IX+d)",
25f7f1b0 [glm-5.2] #			"length": 3,
25f7f1b0 [glm-5.2] #			"operands": ["A", "(IX+d)"]
25f7f1b0 [glm-5.2] #		}
25f7f1b1 [glm-5.2] #	]
25f7f1b1 [glm-5.2] #}
25f7f1b1 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1b1 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1b1 [glm-5.2] #• Benefits of this approach:*
25f7f1b2 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1b2 [glm-5.2] #1. Explicit Operand Mapping: If you are writing an assembler, parsing `ADC A, B` means you
25f7f1b2 [glm-5.2] #just look for a form where `operands` matches `["A", "B"]`. You instantly get your opcodes.
25f7f1b2 [glm-5.2] #2. Instruction Length: The `length` field tells the disassembler exactly how many bytes to
25f7f1b3 [glm-5.2] #read from memory, which is crucial for handling prefixed instructions properly (e.g., `DD 8E d` is
25f7f1b3 [glm-5.2] #3 bytes, while `8E` is 1 byte).
25f7f1b3 [glm-5.2] #3. Dynamic Value Placement: If you eventually add fields to define where the `d`, `n`, or
25f7f1b4 [glm-5.2] #`nn` values are inserted into the opcode stream, having a structured object makes this much easier.
25f7f1b4 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f1b4 [glm-5.2] #This structure keeps all the logic for a single mnemonic contained within its own block, rather
25f7f1b4 [glm-5.2] #than having to cross-reference back to the flat `encoding` table.
25f7f1b5 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f793 [nitro@ryzen5] i prefer space sepearated string over arrays for the inner fields, what are pros and cons?
25f7f8ac [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.018 29.59KB 17.06s
25f7f8ad [glm-5.2] #Using space-separated strings instead of arrays is a perfectly valid approach, especially for a
25f7f8ad [glm-5.2] #specification file that might be manually edited or read frequently. Both methods have their
25f7f8ad [glm-5.2] #trade-offs depending on how your parser is built.
25f7f8ad [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8ae [glm-5.2] #Here are the pros and cons of using space-separated strings for fields like `"opcodes"` or
25f7f8ae [glm-5.2] #`"operands"`:
25f7f8ae [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8ae [glm-5.2] #Pros of Space-Separated Strings
25f7f8ae [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8af [glm-5.2] #1. Readability and Compactness: It visually closer matches how opcodes are typically
25f7f8af [glm-5.2] #written in documentation (e.g., `"ED 4A"`). It reduces JSON clutter (no extra quotes or brackets),
25f7f8af [glm-5.2] #making the file less noisy.
25f7f8af [glm-5.2] #2. Ease of Manual Editing: If you are hand-typing this JSON, typing `"DD 8E"` is faster and
25f7f8b0 [glm-5.2] #less error-prone than typing `["DD", "8E"]`.
25f7f8b0 [glm-5.2] #3. Slightly Smaller File Size: While negligible for a single CPU spec, removing the array
25f7f8b0 [glm-5.2] #brackets and extra quotes does reduce the overall byte count.
25f7f8b1 [glm-5.2] #4. Direct String Matching: If you are disassembling, you can read bytes from memory,
25f7f8b1 [glm-5.2] #convert them to a hex string, and do a direct lookup against the string (e.g., comparing `"ED4A"`
25f7f8b1 [glm-5.2] #or `"ED 4A"`).
25f7f8b1 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8b2 [glm-5.2] #Cons of Space-Separated Strings
25f7f8b2 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8b2 [glm-5.2] #1. Parsing Overhead: Every time your engine reads an entry, it has to split the string
25f7f8b2 [glm-5.2] #(e.g., `"ED 4A".split(" ")`) to get the individual bytes. With an array, the JSON parser already
25f7f8b3 [glm-5.2] #did this for you.
25f7f8b3 [glm-5.2] #2. Fragility to Typos: It is easy to accidentally introduce double spaces (`"ED  4A"`) or
25f7f8b3 [glm-5.2] #trailing spaces. If your parser uses strict splitting, this will cause lookup failures unless you
25f7f8b3 [glm-5.2] #also trim and filter empty strings.
25f7f8b4 [glm-5.2] #3. Schema Validation: If you ever use tools like JSON Schema to validate your file,
25f7f8b4 [glm-5.2] #validating that a string contains exactly two space-separated hex bytes is much harder than
25f7f8b4 [glm-5.2] #validating an array of two hex strings.
25f7f8b5 [glm-5.2] #4. Ambiguity in Operands: For `"operands"`, a string like `"A (IX+d)"` is fine, but if you
25f7f8b5 [glm-5.2] #ever need to represent multiple distinct operands as a list, you lose the clear semantic boundary
25f7f8b5 [glm-5.2] #that an array provides.
25f7f8b5 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8b6 [glm-5.2] #Summary
25f7f8b6 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8b6 [glm-5.2] #If your primary goal is human readability and the JSON is meant to be a clean reference
25f7f8b6 [glm-5.2] #document that your engine parses once at startup, space-separated strings are an excellent choice.
25f7f8b7 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8b7 [glm-5.2] #If your primary goal is machine processing and strict validation, arrays are generally
25f7f8b7 [glm-5.2] #safer.
25f7f8b7 [glm-5.2] #
25f7f8b8 [glm-5.2] #Given your current `encoding` block already uses string keys for opcodes (e.g., `"ED 4A"`),
25f7f8b8 [glm-5.2] #sticking with space-separated strings in the `forms` object will keep your data style consistent.
25f7f8b8 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fcb1 [nitro@ryzen5] will i need to  multiple distinct operands as a list
25f7fde4 [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.020 32.38KB 18.29s
25f7fde4 [glm-5.2] #You will likely not need to use a list for operands in the Z80, because the architecture
25f7fde4 [glm-5.2] #has a strict limit of two operands per instruction (e.g., `LD B, C` or `BIT 3, B`).
25f7fde4 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fde4 [glm-5.2] #If you prefer strings, you can simply use a comma to separate them, which naturally matches
25f7fde5 [glm-5.2] #standard assembly syntax.
25f7fde5 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fde5 [glm-5.2] #For example:
25f7fde5 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fde6 [glm-5.2] #{
25f7fde6 [glm-5.2] #	"opcodes": "88",
25f7fde6 [glm-5.2] #	"syntax": "ADC A,B",
25f7fde7 [glm-5.2] #	"operands": "A,B"
25f7fde7 [glm-5.2] #}
25f7fde7 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fde7 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fde8 [glm-5.2] #• When a string works perfectly:*
25f7fde8 [glm-5.2] #As long as your operand tokens do not contain spaces, a comma-separated string is very easy for a
25f7fde8 [glm-5.2] #parser to split (e.g., `operands.split(",")`). Tokens like `B`, `(HL)`, `(IX+d)`, and `n` are all
25f7fde8 [glm-5.2] #single words, so this approach is clean and reliable.
25f7fde9 [glm-5.2] #
25f7fde9 [glm-5.2] #• The one caveat to watch for:*
25f7fde9 [glm-5.2] #If you ever define operands that contain spaces, such as writing `(IX + d)` instead of `(IX+d)`,
25f7fde9 [glm-5.2] #splitting the string becomes slightly more complex because you have to parse the parentheses rather
25f7fdea [glm-5.2] #than just splitting by comma. As long as you enforce a "no spaces inside operands" rule in your
25f7fdea [glm-5.2] #spec, a comma-separated string will serve you perfectly well.
25f7fdea [glm-5.2] #
