25f80f16 [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.024 38.87KB 17.72s
25f80f16 [glm-5.2] #Using an array for the `forms` is a very suitable idea, especially if order matters to you.
25f80f16 [glm-5.2] #However, switching to an object keyed by the opcode string is also an excellent approach and aligns
25f80f17 [glm-5.2] #closely with how you structured your main `encoding` block.
25f80f17 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f17 [glm-5.2] #Here is a comparison of the two approaches for your `forms` field:
25f80f18 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f18 [glm-5.2] #1. The Array Form (Current)
25f80f18 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f18 [glm-5.2] #"forms": [
25f80f19 [glm-5.2] #	{ "opcodes": "8F", "syntax": "ADC A,A" },
25f80f19 [glm-5.2] #	{ "opcodes": "88", "syntax": "ADC A,B" }
25f80f19 [glm-5.2] #]
25f80f19 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f1a [glm-5.2] #• Pros:*
25f80f1a [glm-5.2] #• Ordering: You can explicitly group and order the forms (e.g., listing all `ADC A,r` forms
25f80f1a [glm-5.2] #first, then `ADC A,n`, then `ADC HL,rr`). This is nice for human readability and documentation
25f80f1a [glm-5.2] #generation.
25f80f1b [glm-5.2] #• Extensibility: If you ever decide to add metadata back to specific forms (like cycle
25f80f1b [glm-5.2] #counts or undocumented flags), you already have objects in place to do so.
25f80f1b [glm-5.2] #
25f80f1c [glm-5.2] #• Cons:*
25f80f1c [glm-5.2] #• More verbose. Your parser has to iterate through the array to find a matching opcode.
25f80f1c [glm-5.2] #
25f80f1c [glm-5.2] #2. The Object Form (Keyed by Opcodes)
25f80f1d [glm-5.2] #
25f80f1d [glm-5.2] #"forms": {
25f80f1d [glm-5.2] #	"8F": "ADC A,A",
25f80f1d [glm-5.2] #	"88": "ADC A,B",
25f80f1e [glm-5.2] #	"DD 8E": "ADC A,(IX+d)",
25f80f1e [glm-5.2] #	"ED 4A": "ADC HL,BC"
25f80f1e [glm-5.2] #}
25f80f1e [glm-5.2] #
25f80f1f [glm-5.2] #• Pros:*
25f80f1f [glm-5.2] #• Extremely Compact: Removes the need for nested objects and repeated `opcodes`/`syntax`
25f80f1f [glm-5.2] #keys.
25f80f20 [glm-5.2] #• Fast Lookups: If you are disassembling, you read the bytes from memory, construct the key
25f80f20 [glm-5.2] #string (e.g., `"DD 8E"`), and instantly access the syntax string via `instruction.forms[key]`.
25f80f20 [glm-5.2] #• Guarantees Uniqueness: An object naturally prevents you from accidentally defining the
25f80f20 [glm-5.2] #same opcode twice.
25f80f21 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f21 [glm-5.2] #• Cons:*
25f80f21 [glm-5.2] #• No Guaranteed Order: While modern JSON parsers typically preserve insertion order, JSON
25f80f21 [glm-5.2] #specification itself does not guarantee object key ordering. If you rely on a specific sequence,
25f80f22 [glm-5.2] #this could be a minor issue.
25f80f22 [glm-5.2] #• Less Extensible: If you want to add cycle counts later, you'd have to change the value
25f80f22 [glm-5.2] #from a string back to an object, like `"8F": { "syntax": "ADC A,A", "cycles": 4 }`.
25f80f22 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f23 [glm-5.2] #Summary
25f80f23 [glm-5.2] #If your goal is maximum compactness and direct opcode-to-syntax mapping (which is great for a
25f80f23 [glm-5.2] #disassembler), the Object Form keyed by opcodes is arguably the better choice.
25f80f24 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f24 [glm-5.2] #If you value strict ordering and might want to add per-form metadata later without changing the
25f80f24 [glm-5.2] #structure, the Array Form is the way to go.
25f80f24 [glm-5.2] #
25f80f24 [glm-5.2] #Given your preference for compactness and space-separated strings shown so far, the object form
25f80f25 [glm-5.2] #would look remarkably clean.
25f80f25 [glm-5.2] #
25f813f0 [nitro@ryzen5] can cycles be deduced from address mode alone?
25f814fb [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.023 41.56KB 16.05s
25f814fb [glm-5.2] #No, cycles cannot be deduced from the address mode alone. While the address mode is a major factor,
25f814fb [glm-5.2] #the timing in a Z80 is determined by a combination of the instruction being executed, the specific
25f814fb [glm-5.2] #registers involved, and the address mode.
25f814fb [glm-5.2] #
25f814fc [glm-5.2] #Here are a few examples from the official Z80 timing tables that demonstrate why address mode alone
25f814fc [glm-5.2] #isn't enough:
25f814fc [glm-5.2] #
25f814fd [glm-5.2] #• 1. Same Address Mode, Different Instructions*
25f814fd [glm-5.2] #If we look at the "Register Indirect" address mode using `(HL)`:
25f814fd [glm-5.2] #• `LD A, (HL)` takes 7 clock cycles (T-states).
25f814fd [glm-5.2] #• `INC (HL)` takes 11 clock cycles.
25f814fe [glm-5.2] #• `RLC (HL)` (via the CB prefix) takes 15 clock cycles.
25f814fe [glm-5.2] #
25f814fe [glm-5.2] #The CPU has to do different amounts of work (fetching, executing, writing back) depending on what
25f814fe [glm-5.2] #the instruction actually does, even if the way it gets the data is the same.
25f814ff [glm-5.2] #
25f814ff [glm-5.2] #• 2. Same Address Mode, Different Registers*
25f814ff [glm-5.2] #If we look at the "Extended" address mode (reading from a specific 16-bit memory address `nn`):
25f814ff [glm-5.2] #• `LD A, (nn)` takes 13 clock cycles.
25f81500 [glm-5.2] #• `LD HL, (nn)` takes 16 clock cycles.
25f81500 [glm-5.2] #• `LD BC, (nn)` takes 20 clock cycles.
25f81500 [glm-5.2] #
25f81501 [glm-5.2] #Even though the address mode is identical, moving data into an 8-bit accumulator is faster than
25f81501 [glm-5.2] #moving 16-bit data into a register pair, and HL has dedicated hardware shortcuts that BC/DE/SP do
25f81501 [glm-5.2] #not, making HL operations slightly faster.
25f81501 [glm-5.2] #
25f81502 [glm-5.2] #• 3. Same Address Mode, Different Contexts*
25f81502 [glm-5.2] #In the "Register" address mode:
25f81502 [glm-5.2] #• `LD B, C` takes 4 clock cycles.
25f81502 [glm-5.2] #• `ADD HL, BC` takes 11 clock cycles.
25f81503 [glm-5.2] #• `EX DE, HL` takes 4 clock cycles.
25f81503 [glm-5.2] #
25f81503 [glm-5.2] #• Conclusion for your JSON spec:*
25f81503 [glm-5.2] #If you plan to include cycle counts (often split into M-cycles and T-states for accurate Z80
25f81504 [glm-5.2] #emulation), you will need to store them at the individual form level. You cannot reliably calculate
25f81504 [glm-5.2] #them just by looking up a property on the address mode.
25f81504 [glm-5.2] #
