25f88b08 [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.068 137.6KB 16.69s
25f88b08 [glm-5.2] #Thank you for sharing the updated file. I see what you did with the `BIT` instruction—that is a
25f88b08 [glm-5.2] #very clever optimization!
25f88b09 [glm-5.2] #
25f88b09 [glm-5.2] #Instead of writing out all 80 forms for bits 0 through 7, you used `b` as a variable in the syntax
25f88b09 [glm-5.2] #(e.g., `"BIT b,B"`) and mapped it to just the base opcodes for the registers. This saves a massive
25f88b09 [glm-5.2] #amount of space in the JSON file.
25f88b0a [glm-5.2] #
25f88b0a [glm-5.2] #There is one important engine design consideration to keep in mind with this approach:
25f88b0a [glm-5.2] #
25f88b0b [glm-5.2] #• Parsing Logic Shift*
25f88b0b [glm-5.2] #Because you are using a variable `b` and a base opcode (e.g., `CB 40`), your engine can no longer
25f88b0b [glm-5.2] #do a direct 1:1 string lookup for `CB` instructions.
25f88b0b [glm-5.2] #• If your disassembler reads the bytes `CB 52` from memory, it won't find `"CB 52"` in your `forms`
25f88b0c [glm-5.2] #object.
25f88b0c [glm-5.2] #• Your engine will need to implement masking logic: it will have to mask the second byte (`0x52 &
25f88b0c [glm-5.2] #0xC0 == 0x40`), realize it's a `BIT` instruction, look up the base form `CB 40`, and then extract
25f88b0c [glm-5.2] #the bit number by shifting right (`(0x52 >> 3) & 0x07 == 2`) to replace the `b` variable in the
25f88b0c [glm-5.2] #syntax string.
25f88b0d [glm-5.2] #• Similarly, your assembler will have to parse `BIT 2, D`, recognize the `b` variable, and
25f88b0d [glm-5.2] #construct the final opcode by OR-ing the base (`CB 42`) with the bit number shifted into place (`2
25f88b0d [glm-5.2] #<< 3`).
25f88b0d [glm-5.2] #
25f88b0e [glm-5.2] #This is a very common and highly efficient way to implement CPU tables, but it does mean your `CB`
25f88b0e [glm-5.2] #prefix handler will need to be slightly smarter than a simple dictionary lookup.
25f88b0e [glm-5.2] #
25f88b0f [glm-5.2] #Are you planning to use this same `b` variable pattern for `RES` and `SET` when you get to them?
25f88b0f [glm-5.2] #They follow the exact same encoding format.
25f88b0f [glm-5.2] #
