25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.018 998B 44.93s
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #Thanks for sharing the layout. Reading the array as eight rows of eight columns and
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #cross‑referencing against the standard C64 matrix, most of the alphanumeric and function‑key cells
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #line up as expected.
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #A couple of things stand out that may be worth a second look, in case they relate to keys that
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #aren't triggering for you:
25fe1673 [glm-5.2] #
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #- Row 0, col 2 is `0x27` (VK_RIGHT). On the C64 matrix that position is Right Shift, which
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #is usually `0xA1` (VK_RSHIFT). VK_RIGHT currently lives here, and VK_DOWN (`0x28`) is in col 7, so
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #the two cursor arrows are split across the row.
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #- Row 6 contains duplicates of codes already used elsewhere: `0x74` (F5), `0x70` (F1) and
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #`0xBB` (OEM_PLUS) all reappear. The canonical C64 row 6 is `£, *, ;, HOME, =, ↑/PI, /, RUN‑STOP`,
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #so this row looks like it may have drifted — for example `0x1B` (Escape) is in col 2 where `;`
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #would be expected, and `0x24` (Home) is in col 4 where `=` would be expected.
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #- Row 7, col 1 is `0xDF`. The C64 cell there is the left‑arrow key; depending on your host
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #mapping you may want something like `0xBA` (OEM_1) or a dedicated code, since `0xDF` is rarely
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #produced by typical keyboards.
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #- Row 7, col 7 is `0x03` (VK_CANCEL/Break). RESTORE on the C64 isn't actually part of the
25fe1674 [glm-5.2] #8×8 matrix — it's wired to the NMI line — so if this cell is meant to represent RUN/STOP, the code
