260ec7b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. What is the `0xBA` key?
260ec7b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In the standard Windows Virtual-Key code table:
260ec7b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `0xBA` (`186`) is `VK_OEM_1`.
260ec7b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• On a standard US keyboard, this is the `;:` key (Semicolon / Colon).
260ec7b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• On some European layouts (like UK), it represents the `´ ~` key or miscellaneous
260ec7b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #punctuation.
260ec7b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
260ec7b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7b7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Why `0xA0` and `0xA1` Are Not Working in Your Array
260ec7b7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7b7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #There are two distinct reasons `0xA0` and `0xA1` might fail depending on how your code handles
260ec7b7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #input:
260ec7b7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7b8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Reason A: `0xA0`/`0xA1` vs. `0x10` (`VK_SHIFT`)
260ec7b8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If your keyboard input listener listens to standard browser events (`keydown`/`keyup`) or standard
260ec7b8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Win32 `WM_KEYDOWN` messages:
260ec7b8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• The system sends `0x10` (`VK_SHIFT`) by default for both shift keys, not `0xA0`
260ec7b8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #or `0xA1`.
260ec7b9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `0xA0` (`VK_LSHIFT`) and `0xA1` (`VK_RSHIFT`) are only emitted if you use low-level keyboard
260ec7b9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #hooks or specifically translate them.
260ec7b9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Fix: If your input handler sends `0x10`, but your matrix only has `0xA0` and `0xA1`, the
260ec7b9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Shift key will never match.
260ec7ba [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7ba [gemini-3.7-flash] #Reason B: The Keys Were Mapped to the Wrong VIC-20 Matrix Slots
260ec7ba [gemini-3.7-flash] #Looking at the VIC-20 hardware matrix (VIA #1 / VIA #2):
260ec7ba [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7ba [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Left Shift is physically wired to Row 3, Column 1.
260ec7bb [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Right Shift is physically wired to Row 4, Column 6.
260ec7bb [gemini-3.7-flash] #• RUN/STOP is physically wired to Row 7, Column 7 (or Row 3, Column 0 depending on
260ec7bb [gemini-3.7-flash] #bit polarity).
260ec7bb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7bc [gemini-3.7-flash] #In your original array:
260ec7bc [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Row 4 started with `0xBA` where RUN/STOP should have been.
260ec7bc [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Row 5 ended with `0xBA` where Function Key (F1) should have been.
260ec7bc [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7bc [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
260ec7bd [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7bd [gemini-3.7-flash] #The Recommended Array
260ec7bd [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7bd [gemini-3.7-flash] #Here is the exact corrected array.
260ec7be [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ec7be [gemini-3.7-flash] #If your input pipeline sends `0x10` for both shifts instead of distinguishing left/right, you can
260ec7be [gemini-3.7-flash] #replace both `0xA0` and `0xA1` with `0x10`:
260ec7be [gemini-3.7-flash] #
