260ef43b [gemini-3.7-flash] #Testing bit 15 (the most significant bit / `0x8000`) of the return value from
260ef43b [gemini-3.7-flash] #`GetAsyncKeyState` is the correct and standard way to determine if a key is currently held down.
260ef43c [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ef43c [gemini-3.7-flash] #Why this is effective for your matrix:
260ef43c [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ef43c [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Left vs. Right Support:
260ef43c [gemini-3.7-flash] #   Unlike standard window messages, `GetAsyncKeyState` properly distinguishes between left and
260ef43d [gemini-3.7-flash] #right modifier keys. This means `VK_LSHIFT` (`0xA0`), `VK_RSHIFT` (`0xA1`), `VK_LCONTROL` (`0xA2`),
260ef43d [gemini-3.7-flash] #and `VK_LMENU` (`0xA4` / Left Alt) will all work independently when queried with this function.
260ef43d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ef43d [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Standard Implementation Check:
260ef43e [gemini-3.7-flash] #   Since `GetAsyncKeyState` returns a 16-bit signed integer (`SHORT`), the most reliable checks
260ef43e [gemini-3.7-flash] #are:
260ef43e [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Bitmask: `(GetAsyncKeyState(vKey) & 0x8000) != 0`
260ef43e [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Sign comparison: `GetAsyncKeyState(vKey) < 0`
260ef43f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260ef43f [gemini-3.7-flash] #• (Note: You can safely ignore bit 0 of the return value, as its state tracking for whether the key
260ef43f [gemini-3.7-flash] #was pressed since the last query is unreliable across modern Windows versions).*
260ef43f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
