260f6f36 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.007 12.10KB 3.85s
260f6f36 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The Motorola 6800 specification is well-structured for building a table-driven emulator or
260f6f37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #disassembler.
260f6f37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260f6f37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #A few observations on the structure and details provided:
260f6f37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260f6f37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Specification Mapping (`specprofile`)
260f6f37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• The `specprofile` table cleanly captures the variability in byte lengths and execution cycles
260f6f38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #across the addressing modes (Immediate, Direct, Indexed, Extended) and dual accumulator variants (A
260f6f38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #vs. B).
260f6f38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Grouping instructions by `spec` index allows decoder tables to dynamically derive both
260f6f38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #instruction length and cycle costs without extensive `switch` statements.
260f6f39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260f6f39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Interrupt Vectors
260f6f39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• The vector table correctly defines the standard M6800 big-endian hardware interrupt addresses:
260f6f39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * `RESET`: `FFFE–FFFF`
260f6f3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * `NMI`: `FFFC–FFFD`
260f6f3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * `SWI`: `FFFA–FFFB`
260f6f3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * `IRQ`: `FFF8–FFF9`
260f6f3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260f6f3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Register Architecture & Sizing
260f6f3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Accumulators & Indexing: The physical 6800 has two 8-bit accumulators (`A` and `B`), a
260f6f3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #16-bit index register (`X`), a 16-bit stack pointer (`SP`), a 16-bit program counter (`PC`), and an
260f6f3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #8-bit condition code register (`CCR`).
260f6f3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #• In your TypeScript skeleton, `registers` is allocated as `Uint8Array(10)` and `pc` as
260f6f3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #`Uint16Array(4)`. Depending on your indexing strategy, you can either map these directly to typed
260f6f3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #offsets (e.g., viewing 16-bit registers like `SP` and `X` via a `DataView` or separate
260f6f3d [gemini-3.7-flash] #`Uint16Array` elements) or use dedicated properties for clearer register access during execution
260f6f3d [gemini-3.7-flash] #cycles.
260f6f3d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
260f6f3d [gemini-3.7-flash] #Please let me know if you would like to proceed with implementing specific decoding logic,
260f6f3e [gemini-3.7-flash] #condition code calculations, or cycle-accurate execution routines for the `tick()` method.
260f6f3e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
