262232ab [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.010 691B 9.85s
262232ab [gemini-3.7-flash] #In the Commodore PET ROM interrupt routine, `0104,X` is an indexed access into Page 1 (the 6502
262232ab [gemini-3.7-flash] #hardware stack) to inspect the saved Processor Status register (P$).
262232ab [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232ac [gemini-3.7-flash] #How the Stack is Structured
262232ac [gemini-3.7-flash] #When a hardware interrupt or a `BRK` occurs, the following sequence takes place:
262232ac [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232ac [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. The 6502 hardware automatically pushes:
262232ad [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Program Counter High (`PC_hi`)
262232ad [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Program Counter Low (`PC_lo`)
262232ad [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Processor Status (`P`)
262232ad [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. The ROM IRQ service routine immediately preserves registers:
262232ae [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Pushes `A`
262232ae [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Pushes `X`
262232ae [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Pushes `Y`
262232ae [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. It copies the stack pointer with `TSX` and executes `LDA $0104,X`.
262232af [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232af [gemini-3.7-flash] #Because the stack pointer points to the next empty location, offset `$0104,X` resolves directly to
262232af [gemini-3.7-flash] #the saved Processor Status byte pushed during step 1:
262232af [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `$0101,X` = Saved `Y`
262232b0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `$0102,X` = Saved `X`
262232b0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `$0103,X` = Saved `A`
262232b0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `$0104,X` = Saved `P` (Status Register)
262232b1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `$0105,X` = Saved `PC_lo`
262232b1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `$0106,X` = Saved `PC_hi`
262232b1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262232b2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Why the Check Happens
262232b2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Both hardware `IRQ`s and the software `BRK` instruction share the interrupt vector at `FFFE/FFFF`.
262232b2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The ROM checks the pushed status byte to determine which event occurred:
262232b3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Hardware IRQ: Pushes P with Bit 4 (B flag) = 0 and Bit 5 = 1.
262232b3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• BRK / PHP: Pushes P with Bit 4 (B flag) = 1 and Bit 5 = 1.
262232b3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The ROM executes `AND #$10` (checking bit 4) to branch to the monitor/break handler if `BRK` was
262232b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #executed, or proceed with timer and keyboard scanning if it was a hardware IRQ.
262232b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262232b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Likely Cause of the Crash
262232b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If your simulator sets Bit 4 to 1 when pushing the status register during a hardware `IRQ`,
262232b5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #the PET kernel misidentifies the timer interrupt as a software break (`BRK`), redirecting execution
262232b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #into the machine language monitor or warm-start routine rather than returning with `RTI`.
262232b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262232b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Ensuring that hardware interrupts clear Bit 4 when pushing the status register to the stack should
262232b6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #resolve the issue.
262232b7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
