2622632c [gemini-3.7-flash] #In your simulator, handling a write to `$E810` (PIA 1, Port A) depends on standard 6520 PIA
2622632c [gemini-3.7-flash] #register addressing and how the PET hardware is wired.
2622632c [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2622632c [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2622632d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2622632d [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Check the PIA Control Register (`$E811`)
2622632d [gemini-3.7-flash] #The 6520 PIA uses bit 2 of its Control Register A (`CRA` at `E811`) to determine whether register
2622632d [gemini-3.7-flash] #`E810` targets the Data Direction Register or the Output Register:
2622632e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2622632e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• If `CRA` Bit 2 is `0`: The write updates `DDRA` (Data Direction Register A).
2622632e [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * A `0` bit configures the corresponding pin as an input.
2622632e [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * A `1` bit configures the corresponding pin as an output.
2622632e [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * During normal PET operation, the ROM sets `DDRA` to `$00` (all 8 lines as inputs).
2622632f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2622632f [gemini-3.7-flash] #• If `CRA` Bit 2 is `1`: The write updates `ORA` (Output Register A).
2622632f [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Stores the byte in the internal output register latch.
2622632f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26226330 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26226330 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26226330 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. External Hardware Effects on the PET
26226330 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• No Peripheral Side-Effects: Unlike PIA 1 Port B (which drives the keyboard row decoder)
26226331 [gemini-3.7-flash] #or PIA 2 / VIA (which drive video, sound, and IEEE-488), PIA 1 Port A lines are wired only to the
26226331 [gemini-3.7-flash] #keyboard matrix columns via pull-up resistors.
26226331 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Latch Storage Only: When the PET ROM writes to `$E810` while configured as outputs or
26226331 [gemini-3.7-flash] #during initialization, you only need to store the written byte into the internal `ORA` state
26226332 [gemini-3.7-flash] #variable. No external components need to be notified.
26226332 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26226332 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26226332 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26226333 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Impact on Subsequent Reads
26226333 [gemini-3.7-flash] #When the 6502 reads `$E810` (with `CRA` bit 2 = 1):
26226333 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26226333 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• For any bit where `DDRA` is `0` (Input): Return the keyboard line state (defaults to `1`
26226334 [gemini-3.7-flash] #from pull-ups; goes to `0` if a key on the active row is closed).
26226334 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• For any bit where `DDRA` is `1` (Output): Return the bit value previously stored in
26226334 [gemini-3.7-flash] #`ORA`.
26226334 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26226335 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In practice, since the PET kernel configures all Port A pins as inputs (`DDRA = $00`), reads will
26226335 [gemini-3.7-flash] #almost always reflect the keyboard matrix rather than the value written to `ORA`.
26226335 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
