2625939a [nitro@ryzen5] is commodore pet monochrome only?
262593ee [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 498B 4.41s
262593ee [gemini-3.7-flash] #Yes, the Commodore PET was monochrome only.
262593ee [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262593ef [gemini-3.7-flash] #All production models in the PET (and later CBM) series were designed with integrated monochrome
262593ef [gemini-3.7-flash] #displays and lacked native hardware support for color graphics.
262593ef [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262593ef [gemini-3.7-flash] #Depending on the specific model and production run, the displays used different phosphor colors:
262593f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262593f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• White Phosphor: Used in the earliest PET 2001 models (9-inch integrated screens).
262593f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Green Phosphor: The most common variety, found on later 9-inch and 12-inch models (such
262593f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #as the 4000 and 8000 series).
262593f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Amber Phosphor: Offered primarily in European markets for the later business models to
262593f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #reduce eye strain.
262593f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262593f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The video hardware in the PET generated a single-bit digital video signal aimed directly at the
262593f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #internal monitor circuitry (or an external monochrome monitor on models without a built-in screen).
262593f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262593f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #While a few third-party expansion boards existed that could tap into the bus to output composite or
262593f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #RGB color video, out of the box, the machine remained strictly monochrome. Commodore did not
262593f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #introduce native color support in their personal computer line until the release of the VIC-20 in
262593f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1980/1981.
262593f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2625979c [nitro@ryzen5] so text was black on white unless reverse bit is on?
262597d6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.002 1.882KB 3.23s
262597d6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #It was actually the opposite by default: light text on a dark (black) background.
262597d6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262597d6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Here is how the display logic worked:
262597d7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262597d7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Normal Mode (Default): The CRT screen background remained unlit (black), and the electron
262597d7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #beam lit up the individual phosphor pixels to draw the character. On a white-phosphor model, this
262597d7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #produced white text on a black background.
262597d8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Reverse Mode (Bit 7 Set): Setting the highest bit of a character's screen code inverted
262597d8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #the pixel output. This drew an illuminated solid block with the character itself carved out of
262597d8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #unlit (black) pixels, producing dark text on a bright rectangular background.
262597d9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262597d9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In BASIC, users could toggle this reverse state using the `RVS ON` and `RVS OFF` keys, or by
262597d9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #printing control codes such as `CHR(18)` (reverse on) and `CHR(146)` (reverse off).
262597d9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
