262b6da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #This trace shows a clean, standard exit from the interrupt handler with no keys detected:
262b6da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262b6da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Breakdown of the Sequence
262b6da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262b6da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Row Advance (`$E70F`):
262b6da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * `INC $E810` increments the row select nibble to prepare for scanning the next keyboard row (or
262b6da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #rolls over when done).
262b6da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262b6da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Debounce & Matrix State Tracking (`E714–E720`):
262b6da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * `LDA 0223` reads `FF` (the "no key currently pressed" sentinel value set at the start
262b6da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #of the scan).
262b6da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * `CMP 0203` compares this against the previous frame's key state (`00`).
262b6da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * `STA 0203` records `FF` as the new previous key state (signaling all keys are
262b6da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #released).
262b6da8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * `TAX` / `BMI E73C` tests the high bit of the value. Since `FF` is negative (`BMI` =
262b6da8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Branch on Minus), it branches directly to `$E73C`, bypassing the key-buffering queue.
262b6da8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262b6da8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. IRQ Exit & Context Restore (`E67E–E683`):
262b6da9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Registers are popped from the stack in reverse order:
262b6da9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * `PLA` \rightarrow `TAY` (Restores Y)
262b6da9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * `PLA` \rightarrow `TAX` (Restores X)
262b6da9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * `PLA` (Restores A)
262b6daa [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * `RTI` restores the Processor Status register and Program Counter, returning execution
262b6daa [gemini-3.7-flash] #to the main system loop.
262b6daa [gemini-3.7-flash] #
