262bd668 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd668 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Where We Are in the Boot Sequence
262bd668 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Tracing back through the flow:
262bd669 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Reset / Editor Init: Set cursor pointers to Row 0, Col 0 (`$8000`).
262bd669 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. RAM Sizing (`RAMTAS`): Counted RAM up to `$7FFF` (32 KB).
262bd669 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Banner Number Prep (`E15A–E164`): Subtracted start of memory from top of memory ($31,743
262bd669 [gemini-3.7-flash] #bytes free) and called `GIVAYF`/`FOUT`.
262bd66a [gemini-3.7-flash] #4. `FOUT` Formatted the Digits: It converted `31743` to the ASCII string `" 31743"` into
262bd66a [gemini-3.7-flash] #the text buffer at `$0100` (RAM stack page), but has not output it yet.
262bd66a [gemini-3.7-flash] #5. Where the Trace Is Now (`$E167`): It is setting up the string descriptor for `" BYTES
262bd66a [gemini-3.7-flash] #FREE"` before calling the print loop.
262bd66b [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd66b [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262bd66b [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd66b [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. When Does PET Screen RAM Get Written?
262bd66c [gemini-3.7-flash] #The screen memory actually gets written in the next two stages:
262bd66c [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd66c [gemini-3.7-flash] #A. The Clear-Screen Control Code
262bd66d [gemini-3.7-flash] #During the print sequence, the first character sent to `BSOUT` (`FFD2`) is PETSCII `93` (Clear
262bd66d [gemini-3.7-flash] #Screen / CLR).
262bd66d [gemini-3.7-flash] #• When the Screen Editor receives `93`, it runs a fast loop filling 1,000 bytes starting at `8000`
262bd66d [gemini-3.7-flash] #with `$20` (Space character code).
262bd66e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• This wipes out all the `$00` (`@`) bytes.
262bd66e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd66e [gemini-3.7-flash] #B. Writing Characters to Screen Codes
262bd66e [gemini-3.7-flash] #When text is finally streamed character-by-character through `BSOUT`:
262bd66f [gemini-3.7-flash] #• The screen editor takes each PETSCII byte (e.g., `'B'` = `42`), converts it to Screen Code (`'B'`
262bd66f [gemini-3.7-flash] #= `02`), and writes it to `(E0),Y` (where `E0/E1` points to `8000 + (Row \times 40) + Col`).
262bd66f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd66f [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
262bd670 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd670 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Where to Breakpoint to See Screen Memory Change
262bd670 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd670 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If you want to step directly to the screen writes:
262bd671 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262bd671 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. `$E03A` (or your ROM's Clear Screen subroutine):
262bd671 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Watch for loops writing `20` across `8000–$83E7`.
262bd671 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. `E46C` / `E47B` (Screen Editor Character Poke):
262bd672 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * This is where `STA (E0),Y` physically deposits character codes into `8000` video RAM.
262bd672 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. `$E180` (BASIC `READY.` loop):
262bd672 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * By the time execution reaches `$E180`, screen memory will contain:
262bd672 [gemini-3.7-flash] #      Full banner (` COMMODORE BASIC *` or similar depending on ROM version)
262bd673 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * ` 31743 BYTES FREE`
262bd673 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     * `READY.`
262bd673 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
