262c4ca2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #There are a few common reasons why the execution trace indicates writes are occurring at an address
262c4ca2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #(such as `$8328`) while the memory dump shows the data was not modified:
262c4ca2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262c4ca3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Address Decoding and Bus Routing in `writeAddress`
262c4ca3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If `writeAddress(address, value)` contains memory mapping logic (such as bank switching, RAM/ROM
262c4ca3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #tables, or hardware registers):
262c4ca3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Ensure the target range (around `$8000`) is mapped as writable RAM. If the address maps to a ROM
262c4ca4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #area or an unmapped region in your memory controller, `writeAddress` may be silently dropping the
262c4ca4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #value.
262c4ca4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• If `writeAddress` writes to a dedicated video RAM buffer or a banked array instead of the main
262c4ca4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #`this.memory` array, direct inspection of `this.memory` will not reflect the changes.
262c4ca5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262c4ca5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Discrepancy Between Memory Dump Method and Write Method
262c4ca5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• If your memory dump inspects `this.memory[...]` directly rather than invoking your system's read
262c4ca5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #method (e.g. `this.readAddress(...)`), any banking, mirroring, or memory controller translation
262c4ca6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #used during execution will cause the dump to show the wrong physical backing array.
262c4ca6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262c4ca6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Direct Zero-Page Access
262c4ca6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In the opcode implementation, the pointer bytes are read directly from `this.memory[base]` rather
262c4ca7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #than via `this.readAddress(base)` or `this.get8(base)`. If zero-page memory is banked, offset, or
262c4ca7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #mirrored, reading `this.memory` directly might fetch incorrect pointer addresses compared to
262c4ca7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #standard bus reads.
262c4ca7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262c4ca8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #4. Overwriting by Subsequent Operations
262c4ca8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Check if a subsequent interrupt handler, display refresh routine, or DMA cycle runs immediately
262c4ca8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #after this loop and resets the video memory.
262c4ca8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
262c4ca9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If you would like to inspect the bus mapping or write routines, please share how `writeAddress` and
262c4ca9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #the memory dump function access the backing store.
262c4ca9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
