26385ae4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.010 4.106KB 10.00s
26385ae5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #This behavior typically happens because PIA 1 Port B (`$E812`) is returning `0x00` instead of
26385ae5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #`0xFF` when no keys are pressed.
26385ae5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385ae5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Why This Happens in the PET ROM
26385ae6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385ae6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The PET keyboard matrix uses open-collector lines pulled high by pull-up resistors:
26385ae6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Column Drive (PIA 1 Port A / `E810`): The lower nibble drives a 74145 BCD-to-decimal
26385ae7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #decoder to pull one of the 10 keyboard columns low (0–$9).
26385ae7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Row Read (PIA 1 Port B / `$E812`): The 8 row lines are read as inputs.
26385ae7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385ae7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The Kernal keyboard scan routine (`SCNKEY`) works like this:
26385ae8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385ae8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385ae8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    LDX #$00        ; Start at Column 0
26385ae8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #ScanLoop:
26385ae9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    STX $E810       ; Output column index to 74145
26385ae9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    LDA $E812       ; Read row lines (PIA1 Port B)
26385ae9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    CMP #$FF        ; Are ALL lines pulled high (no key pressed)?
26385ae9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    BNE KeyFound    ; If != 0xFF, a key is pressed -> exit loop!
26385aea [gemini-3.7-flash] #    INX
26385aea [gemini-3.7-flash] #    CPX #$0A        ; Done all 10 columns?
26385aea [gemini-3.7-flash] #    BNE ScanLoop
26385aeb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385aeb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385aeb [gemini-3.7-flash] #The Root Cause
26385aeb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385aec [gemini-3.7-flash] #• If your simulator returns `0x00` (default uninitialized memory) on reads to `E812`, the CPU
26385aec [gemini-3.7-flash] #compares `00` against `$FF`.
26385aec [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Because `00 != FF`, the Kernal believes every key in Column 0 is held down
26385aec [gemini-3.7-flash] #simultaneously.
26385aed [gemini-3.7-flash] #• It immediately branches out of the scanning loop on the first iteration without ever incrementing
26385aed [gemini-3.7-flash] #to columns 1–9.
26385aed [gemini-3.7-flash] #• At the end of the scan routine, the Kernal writes `F0` (an invalid BCD value > 9) to `E810` to
26385aed [gemini-3.7-flash] #disable the 74145 decoder outputs.
26385aee [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385aee [gemini-3.7-flash] #How to Fix
26385aee [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26385aee [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Default Floating State: Ensure reads from PIA 1 Port B (`$E812`) return `0xFF` when no
26385aef [gemini-3.7-flash] #keys are pressed (active-low matrix).
26385aef [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Key Press Representation: When a key is pressed at `(column, row)`, pulling that line
26385aef [gemini-3.7-flash] #low should flip the corresponding bit to `0` (i.e., `0xFF & ~(1 << row)`).
26385aef [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Data Direction Check: Verify that `E812` acts as input when Data Direction Register B
26385aef [gemini-3.7-flash] #(DDRB) is `00` (which the PET Kernal sets up during boot).
26385af0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
