263c1b48 [glm-5.3] #Thanks — this is a revised machine.ts, and I can see several of the previously flagged items have
263c1b48 [glm-5.3] #been addressed. A quick diff review:
263c1b48 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b48 [glm-5.3] #• Fixes confirmed*
263c1b49 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b49 [glm-5.3] #- `epoch` is now `Date.UTC(2026,7)` with the comment updated to "August 1 2026" — month indexing
263c1b49 [glm-5.3] #and description now agree.
263c1b49 [glm-5.3] #- `tickVic()` — the `$9111` expression is properly parenthesised, so the VIC-20 joystick port
263c1b4a [glm-5.3] #composition now evaluates as intended.
263c1b4a [glm-5.3] #- `onInput` case `49` now logs `appleSabotage.title` — the ReferenceError is gone.
263c1b4a [glm-5.3] #- `memCode()` now uses `missingCodes.add(...)` — the honest Set API.
263c1b4b [glm-5.3] #- `refreshCRT()` takes `game: Machine` as a parameter instead of referencing an undefined global.
263c1b4b [glm-5.3] #
263c1b4b [glm-5.3] #• New regression in branch()*
263c1b4b [glm-5.3] #
263c1b4c [glm-5.3] #The restructuring introduced a real bug. `const target=(pc + 2 + offset) & 0xFFFF` is now computed
263c1b4c [glm-5.3] #before the `offset -= 256` sign adjustment, which only happens inside `if(taken)` — and
263c1b4c [glm-5.3] #only feeds `branchBackwards()`. Since `get8()` returns an unsigned byte, every taken backward
263c1b4c [glm-5.3] #branch now lands 256 bytes too high.
263c1b4d [glm-5.3] #
263c1b4d [glm-5.3] #Using your own PET idle loop from the trace as the example: at `E29A`, `BEQ E297` encodes offset
263c1b4d [glm-5.3] #`FB` (−5). The new code computes `pc[0] = E29C + FB = E397` instead of `E297` — so the very first
263c1b4e [glm-5.3] #iteration of the main wait loop wanders off into `E397`. Forward branches are unaffected. The
263c1b4e [glm-5.3] #remedy is simply to move the `target` computation back inside `if(taken)`, after the sign extension
263c1b4e [glm-5.3] #(or sign-extend offset once, up front, and derive everything from that).
263c1b4e [glm-5.3] #
263c1b4f [glm-5.3] #The associated display change is otherwise an improvement — returning the true target with `*`
263c1b4f [glm-5.3] #marking not-taken is much clearer than the old fall-through-PC convention.
263c1b4f [glm-5.3] #
263c1b4f [glm-5.3] #• Knock-on from the Set fix*
263c1b50 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b50 [glm-5.3] #`reportMissing()` still uses `Object.keys(missingCodes)`, which returns an empty array for a Set —
263c1b50 [glm-5.3] #so it now silently reports nothing. The old bracket assignment worked with `Object.keys()` by
263c1b50 [glm-5.3] #accident; `.add()` doesn't. Iterating the Set directly (`for(const code of missingCodes)`) would
263c1b50 [glm-5.3] #restore it.
263c1b51 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b51 [glm-5.3] #• On 0xAF*
263c1b51 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b51 [glm-5.3] #Commenting the handler out is a reasonable move — with the table entry still present at index 175,
263c1b52 [glm-5.3] #hitting `AF` now falls through to `fail("unsupported opcode 175 (AF)")` and halts stepping, which
263c1b52 [glm-5.3] #is a safer failure mode than silently misexecuting. One note for when you revisit it: the commented
263c1b52 [glm-5.3] #block is labelled `LAX (Indirect), X` and implemented as 2-byte indirect,X, but `AF` on real
263c1b53 [glm-5.3] #silicon is LAX absolute (3 bytes) — indirect,X is `A3`. The opcode table entry describing
263c1b53 [glm-5.3] #it as a 2-byte LDA would also want correcting at the same time.
263c1b53 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b53 [glm-5.3] #• Still outstanding*
263c1b54 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b54 [glm-5.3] #`displayApple1()` retains `if(frame)` referencing an undefined `frame` — it's unreferenced dead
263c1b54 [glm-5.3] #code at present, but it would throw if ever called.
263c1b54 [glm-5.3] #
263c1b55 [glm-5.3] #Happy to look at anything else — or at how the PET behaves once the branch target fix is in, since
263c1b55 [glm-5.3] #that one will be immediately visible in the trace.
263c1b55 [glm-5.3] #
