26407b2b [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.045 167.5KB 5.86s
26407b2c [gemini-3.7-flash] #Yes, testing `cra & 4` (bit 2 of `$E811`) is the correct way to distinguish between DDRA and the
26407b2c [gemini-3.7-flash] #output register (PRA) on the 6520 PIA.
26407b2c [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b2c [gemini-3.7-flash] #However, there are a few critical hardware details in how the Commodore PET keyboard scanning
26407b2d [gemini-3.7-flash] #actually operates that explain why it may not be working:
26407b2d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b2d [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26407b2d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b2e [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Active-Low Inverted Logic (74145 BCD Decoder & Key Matrix)
26407b2e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b2e [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Hardware Wiring:
26407b2e [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * The PET drives bits 0–3 of PIA1 Port A (`$E810`) into a 74145 BCD-to-decimal decoder.
26407b2f [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * The selected row line is pulled LOW (0); all unselected rows remain HIGH (1).
26407b2f [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * When a key is pressed, it shorts the selected low row line to the corresponding column line
26407b2f [gemini-3.7-flash] #going into PIA1 Port B (`$E812`).
26407b2f [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Matrix Output:
26407b30 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Unpressed keys pull Port B HIGH (`0xFF`) via pull-up resistors.
26407b30 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Pressed keys pull the corresponding bit LOW (`0`).
26407b30 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Summary: A fully open/unpressed keyboard matrix must return `0xFF`, and a pressed key
26407b30 [gemini-3.7-flash] #pulls its bit to `0` (active low). If your matrix returns `0x00` when idle or `1` for pressed keys,
26407b31 [gemini-3.7-flash] #the ROM will either register every key pressed at once or ignore them completely.
26407b31 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b31 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26407b32 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b32 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Output Masking on Read of Port A (`$E810`)
26407b32 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b32 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In your current implementation:
26407b33 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b33 [gemini-3.7-flash] #case 0xe810: // PIA1_PA
26407b33 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    ...
26407b33 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    value |= 0xc0;
26407b34 [gemini-3.7-flash] #    break;
26407b34 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b34 [gemini-3.7-flash] #On the PET:
26407b34 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Bits 0–3 are outputs (keyboard row drive).
26407b35 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• When reading PRA back, the low 4 bits should reflect the value last written to `$E810`.
26407b35 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• If you overwrite or return an uninitialised `mem[address]`, the PET ROM's keyboard debounce
26407b35 [gemini-3.7-flash] #routines (which read `$E810` back to confirm the current row) might read corrupt data.
26407b35 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b36 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26407b36 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b36 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. `$E813` (CRB) CB1/CB2 Frame Blanking Interrupt
26407b36 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The PET keyboard scan is driven by an interrupt handler that triggers on the vertical retrace pulse
26407b37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #wired to PIA1 CB1:
26407b37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b37 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• On real hardware, the 60 Hz vertical blank pulse connects to PIA1 line CB1.
26407b38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• This sets Bit 7 of `E813` (CRB) to `1` and triggers an IRQ (if bit 0 of `E813` is
26407b38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #enabled).
26407b38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Inside the PET Kernal IRQ routine:
26407b38 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  1. It checks if bit 7 of `$E813` is set.
26407b39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  2. It reads `E812` (PRB), which automatically clears bit 7 of `E813`.
26407b39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  3. It executes the keyboard scan routine (`E680` on Basic 4 / `E4B0` on Basic 2).
26407b39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b39 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If the IRQ is generated by a timer rather than asserting bit 7 of `$E813`, the ROM interrupt
26407b3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #handler will skip the keyboard scanning routine entirely.
26407b3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26407b3a [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #Summary Checklist for PET Keyboard
26407b3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26407b3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Row Strobe ($E810): Bits 0–3 select row 0–9.
26407b3b [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Column Return ($E812): Returns `0xFF` when no keys are pressed on that row; bits are
26407b3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #pulled to `0` for pressed keys.
26407b3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Interrupt Flag ($E813): Bit 7 must be set at 50/60 Hz to signal vertical blank and
26407b3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #trigger the Kernal keyboard scan interrupt.
26407b3c [gemini-3.7-flash] #4. Flag Clear on Read: Reading `E812` must clear bit 7 of `E813`.
26407b3d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264087e8 [nitro@ryzen5] is the row strobe triggered by just write or read also?
26408831 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.046 170.6KB 4.09s
26408831 [gemini-3.7-flash] #The row strobe is driven strictly by writes to `E810` (PIA1 Port A). A read from `E810`
26408831 [gemini-3.7-flash] #does not change the active row.
26408832 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408832 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26408832 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408832 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Hardware Flow
26408833 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408833 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Write to `$E810` (Row Select):
26408833 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * The 6502 writes a 4-bit value (0–9) to bits 0–3 of Port A.
26408833 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * This value is latched internally by the 6520 PIA and presented on output pins `PA0`–`PA3`.
26408834 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * These 4 lines feed directly into the 74145 BCD-to-Decimal decoder, which pulls the
26408834 [gemini-3.7-flash] #chosen row low.
26408834 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408834 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Read from `$E812` (Column Sense):
26408835 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * The 6502 reads Port B to check which column lines have been pulled low by pressed keys on that
26408835 [gemini-3.7-flash] #row.
26408835 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408835 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Read from `$E810` (Optional by software):
26408836 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * A read of `$E810` simply samples the PIA pins/output register and returns the value currently
26408836 [gemini-3.7-flash] #on the lines (plus IEEE-488 / Cassette input bits on lines 6–7). It does not alter the latch or
26408836 [gemini-3.7-flash] #change the selected row.
26408836 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408837 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26408837 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408837 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In Your Emulator Code
26408837 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26408838 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• You should only invoke `scanKeyboard(value & 0x0F)` inside `writePET()` when `address === 0xE810`
26408838 [gemini-3.7-flash] #(and `cra & 4 !== 0`).
26408838 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `readPET(0xE810)` should only return the latched value and clear IRQ flags in `$E811` without
26408838 [gemini-3.7-flash] #triggering any row changes.
26408839 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640ac38 [nitro@ryzen5] my trace shows e813 with 35 during kb scan?
2640b7bf [roha] [KOHA] Share file path: C:\nitrologic\arcade\notes.txt  size: 0
2640b8bf [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.089 327.1KB 5.62s
2640b8bf [gemini-3.7-flash] #Yes, seeing `35` (binary `0011 0101`) written to `E813` (`CRB`) during the keyboard scan is
2640b8bf [gemini-3.7-flash] #completely normal and expected behaviour for Commodore PET ROMs.
2640b8c0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Here is the bit breakdown of what the PET Kernal configures with `$35`:
2640b8c0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2640b8c1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Bit Breakdown for `E813` = `35`
2640b8c1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #╭────────────────┬─────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2640b8c2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  Bit           │  Value  │  Meaning on 6520 PIA CRB                                                                                     │
2640b8c2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #├────────────────┼─────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2640b8c2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  :---          │  :---:  │  :---                                                                                                        │
2640b8c3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  **Bit 0**     │  `1`    │  **Enable CB1 Interrupt (IRQ)** — triggers on screen refresh / vertical retrace.                             │
2640b8c3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  **Bit 1**     │  `0`    │  **CB1 Active Edge** — low-to-high transition detection.                                                     │
2640b8c3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  **Bit 2**     │  `1`    │  **Port B Access Mode** — directs `$E812` reads/writes to **PRB** (Keyboard column lines) rather than DDRB.  │
2640b8c3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  **Bits 3–5**  │  `110`  │  **CB2 Output Control** — Cassette motor line / diagnostics handshake.                                       │
2640b8c4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #│  **Bits 6–7**  │  `00`   │  Read-only interrupt flags (cleared when writing).                                                           │
2640b8c4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #╰────────────────┴─────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2640b8c4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2640b8c5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #What This Means for Your Implementation
2640b8c5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Port Selection (`crb & 0x04`):
2640b8c6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Since bit 2 is `1` (`0x35 & 0x04 !== 0`), reading `$E812` correctly routes to
2640b8c6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #`readPetMatrix()` rather than returning `ddrb`.
2640b8c6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Interrupt Flag (`mem[0xE813] & 0x80`):
2640b8c6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * When vertical blank occurs, the PET expects bit 7 of `E813` to be set to 1 (making
2640b8c7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #`E813` read as `B5` or `80 | 0x35`).
2640b8c7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * The PET Kernal IRQ handler checks `LDA $E813`, tests bit 7, and if present, jumps into the
2640b8c7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #keyboard scan routine.
2640b8c7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Clearing Bit 7:
2640b8c8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * When the ROM reads `$E812` during the scan, your code currently performs:
2640b8c8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #     mem[0xE813] &= 0x3F;
2640b8c8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * This preserves the `$35` configuration while clearing the interrupt flag bit 7, matching
2640b8c9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #physical PIA1 behaviour.
2640b8c9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8c9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2640b8ca [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8ca [gemini-3.7-flash] #Key Takeaway
2640b8ca [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8ca [gemini-3.7-flash] #The value `$35` confirms that the ROM has successfully set up PIA1 for active keyboard scanning and
2640b8cb [gemini-3.7-flash] #IRQ generation. If keypresses are still not registering, verify that:
2640b8cb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
2640b8cb [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `readPetMatrix()` returns inverted logic (`0xFF` when idle, active bit pulled to `0` when a key
2640b8cb [gemini-3.7-flash] #is pressed).
2640b8cc [gemini-3.7-flash] #• The 50/60 Hz interrupt handler is firing while bit 7 of `$E813` is asserted.
2640b8cc [gemini-3.7-flash] #
