26416568 [nitro@ryzen5] what do emulators do for shifted numbers?
264165f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.011 16.03KB 8.11s
264165f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Emulators generally take one of two approaches to handle shifted numbers and the differences
264165f0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #between modern PC layouts and the Commodore PET keyboard:
264165f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
264165f1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Symbolic Mapping (Default / Recommended)
264165f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Because modern PC keyboards require `Shift` for `!` and output `1` unshifted, while the original
264165f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Commodore PET graphics keyboard did the exact opposite (unshifted gave symbols like `!`, shifted
264165f2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #gave digits like `1`), symbolic mapping translates the intended character into the correct
264165f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #PET matrix combination.
264165f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In this mode, the emulator automatically modifies the PET shift state regardless of what the user
264165f3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #is physically pressing on the host keyboard:
264165f4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Host types `1` (Unshifted on PC):
264165f4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Emulator detects character `'1'`.
264165f4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * PET matrix requires: Row 0, Col 0 + PET Shift Active.
264165f5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * The emulator artificially activates the Shift bit in the matrix.
264165f5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Host types `Shift + 1` (`!` on PC):
264165f5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * Emulator detects character `'!'`.
264165f6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * PET matrix requires: Row 0, Col 0 + PET Shift Inactive.
264165f6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #  * The emulator suppresses the host shift state and activates only the matrix bit.
264165f6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Common Data Structure for Symbolic Mapping
264165f7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #Instead of storing just raw keycodes, symbolic mapping arrays or lookups often store a tuple or
264165f7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #bitmask indicating the target row, column, and forced shift behavior:
264165f7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #interface PetKeyMapping {
264165f8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #	row: number;
264165f8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #	col: number;
264165f8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #	shiftState: "none" | "forceOn" | "forceOff" | "passThrough";
264165f8 [gemini-3.7-flash] #}
264165f9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165f9 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
264165fa [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165fa [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Positional Mapping (Hardware Accurate)
264165fa [gemini-3.7-flash] #Positional mapping binds the physical key position on a standard PC keyboard directly to the
264165fa [gemini-3.7-flash] #equivalent electrical cross-point on the PET keyboard matrix:
264165fb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165fb [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Host `Key1` always activates Row 0, Col 0.
264165fb [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Host `LeftShift` always activates Row 8, Col 0.
264165fb [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165fc [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Effect:*
264165fc [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Typing unshifted `1` on a PC will output `!` on a PET Graphics ROM.
264165fc [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Typing `Shift + 1` on a PC will output `1` on a PET Graphics ROM.
264165fc [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165fd [gemini-3.7-flash] #This accurately simulates using the original physical hardware switches, but can be awkward for
264165fd [gemini-3.7-flash] #users accustomed to standard typing layouts.
264165fd [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165fe [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
264165fe [gemini-3.7-flash] #
264165fe [gemini-3.7-flash] #Summary of What Most Emulators (like VICE) Do
264165fe [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Configurable Mode: They offer a toggle between Symbolic (translates characters
264165ff [gemini-3.7-flash] #seamlessly) and Positional (1:1 physical switch emulation).
264165ff [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Shift Inversion Logic: For the PET graphics keyboard specifically, symbolic mode inverts
264165ff [gemini-3.7-flash] #the host's shift logic for the top numeric row.
264165ff [gemini-3.7-flash] #3. Keypad Bypass: The numeric keypad on the PET graphics keyboard has dedicated number rows
26416600 [gemini-3.7-flash] #that do not require shift, so emulators often map PC numpad keys directly without shift alteration.
26416600 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416c5c [nitro@ryzen5] instead of backspace i am getting an inverse Q?
26416d9c [gemini-3.7-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.017 18.95KB 19.37s
26416d9d [gemini-3.7-flash] #On Commodore PET systems, seeing an inverse `Q` when you press a key is almost always
26416d9d [gemini-3.7-flash] #caused by one of two things:
26416d9d [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416d9d [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26416d9e [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416d9e [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. The PET is in "Quote Mode" or "Insert Mode"
26416d9e [gemini-3.7-flash] #In PET BASIC / Kernal, control characters do not perform their normal action when the screen editor
26416d9e [gemini-3.7-flash] #is in Quote Mode (after typing `"`) or Insert Mode (after pressing `Shift +
26416d9f [gemini-3.7-flash] #INST/DEL`).
26416d9f [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416d9f [gemini-3.7-flash] #Instead of moving the cursor or deleting, control codes print their reverse-video character
26416d9f [gemini-3.7-flash] #representation:
26416da0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `CRSR DOWN` / `CRSR UP` (PETSCII `11` / `91`) prints visually on screen as an
26416da0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #inverted `Q`.
26416da0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• `INST / DEL` (PETSCII `14` / `94`) prints as an inverted `T`.
26416da0 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If `Shift` is active when `INST/DEL` is triggered, the PET enters Insert Mode, causing
26416da1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #subsequent cursor or control presses to display as inverted characters like inverse `Q`.
26416da1 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26416da2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Duplicate `8` in the Matrix Array
26416da2 [gemini-3.7-flash] #In the matrix array above, `8` (the VK code for `Backspace / DEL`) was placed in two different
26416da3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #positions:
26416da3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Row 1, Col 6 (`INST/DEL`)
26416da3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #• Row 8, Col 5
26416da3 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #If your lookup logic uses `petKeyCodes.indexOf(event.keyCode)` or loops linearly, duplicate numbers
26416da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #or misaligned columns will trigger the wrong matrix row/column (such as triggering the CRSR
26416da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #line instead of the INST/DEL line).
26416da4 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
26416da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da5 [gemini-3.7-flash] #How to Fix
26416da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
26416da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #1. Clean up Row 8: Remove the duplicate `8` from Row 8 (col 5 on Row 8 is not INST/DEL).
26416da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #2. Ensure Unshifted for Delete: Ensure that when the host sends `Backspace` (VK 8), your
26416da6 [gemini-3.7-flash] #emulator clears the PET's `Shift` bit in the matrix. On real PET hardware:
26416da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Unshifted `INST/DEL` = Delete (moves back and deletes character).
26416da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #   * Shifted `INST/DEL` = Insert space (and enters Insert mode).
26416da7 [gemini-3.7-flash] #
