26d29c05 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 49.58KB 50.56s
26d29c05 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #I’ve reviewed the files under `C:\nitrologic\gadget`. The project is an interesting cross-platform
26d29c05 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #ANSI desktop manager built on Deno, with a modular FFI layer for keyboard, mouse, joystick, MIDI,
26d29c05 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #and audio. The code is well‑structured for a prototype, but I’ve noticed several issues that could
26d29c06 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #affect reliability, especially on macOS and Windows.
26d29c06 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c06 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Key observations:
26d29c06 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c07 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Architecture
26d29c07 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  `terminal.ts` provides a unified API on top of platform‑specific backends (Windows vs. macOS).
26d29c07 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #This is a solid pattern for Deno FFI work. The separation of audio, MIDI, and input handling is
26d29c07 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #clear.
26d29c08 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c08 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Mouse button handling
26d29c08 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  In `pollKeypad`, the SGR mouse sequence toggles button bits (`mouseButtons^=1`) instead of
26d29c08 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #explicitly setting/clearing them based on `M` (press) vs. `m` (release). This can cause the button
26d29c09 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #state to become inconsistent if presses/releases are missed or repeated.
26d29c09 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c09 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- macOS MIDI
26d29c09 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  `macosmidi.ts` sets up a callback that only logs to the console via `console.log`, and
26d29c0a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`pollMidi()` always returns an empty array. As a result, MIDI events never appear in the
26d29c0a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`KeypadState.midiEvents` — making MIDI non‑functional from the application’s perspective.
26d29c0a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c0a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Windows MIDI cleanup
26d29c0a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  In `windowsmidi.ts`, the `closeMidi` function uses `for(const midiOut in
26d29c0b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Object.values(midiOutputs))`. `for...in` iterates over keys (strings), not values, so
26d29c0b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`midiOut` becomes a string rather than a pointer. This will cause type errors or undefined
26d29c0b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #behaviour when passed to `midiOutClose`.
26d29c0b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c0c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- macOS audio failure (`-50`)
26d29c0c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  The `AudioQueueNewOutput` failure is likely due to one or more of these factors:
26d29c0c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  - The callback (`noOpCallback`) is not marked `threadSafe`. AudioQueues invoke callbacks on a
26d29c0c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #background thread, so a plain `UnsafeCallback` may not be valid in that context. Use
26d29c0d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`Deno.UnsafeCallback.threadSafe` instead.
26d29c0d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  - The `AudioStreamBasicDescription` may need native‑endian flags or a different format alignment
26d29c0d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #depending on the system’s audio hardware.
26d29c0d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  - Starting the queue immediately after creation (before enqueuing any buffers) can also cause a
26d29c0e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #parameter error on some systems.
26d29c0e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c0e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Audio resource management
26d29c0e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  Both `macosaudio.ts` and `windowsaudio.ts` leak memory: every `playAudio` call allocates
26d29c0f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #buffers/headers that are only stored in an array and never released. Over time, this will exhaust
26d29c0f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #memory. Proper cleanup (unprepare headers, dispose buffers, close queues) is needed.
26d29c0f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c0f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Gadget UI skeleton
26d29c0f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  `gadget.ts` currently doesn’t render anything – `updateConsole` is empty, and the `panel` class
26d29c10 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #is unused. The desktop manager concept is clear, but the drawing/gadget layer is not yet
26d29c10 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #implemented.
26d29c10 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c10 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Minor notes
26d29c11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #  The `KeyBits` enum comments don’t match the actual numeric assignments (TypeScript enums start at
26d29c11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #0, not 1/2/4/8). The comment block is misleading. Also, `QToQuit` is hard‑coded to `false`, so the
26d29c11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`q` key quit path is never active.
26d29c12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d29c12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Overall, the project has a strong foundation but needs a few fixes before it can be considered
26d29c12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #stable across platforms. If you’d like to dig into any specific issue (e.g., the macOS audio error
26d29c12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #or the MIDI callback), I’m happy to help with a focused discussion.
26d29c12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2bd6a [nitro@ryzen5] if mixing wide and normal unicode chars should i use my own isWide method?
26d2beab [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.010 53.05KB 19.60s
26d2beab [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Yes, you should absolutely implement your own width‑detection logic rather than relying on
26d2beac [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`String.length` or a simple `isWide` boolean. For a desktop manager that draws windows, borders,
26d2beac [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #and text, you need to know exactly how many terminal cells a character (or grapheme cluster)
26d2beac [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #occupies. A single boolean isn’t enough because:
26d2beac [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2bead [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Wide chars (CJK, emoji) take 2 cells.
26d2bead [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Narrow chars (ASCII, most punctuation) take 1 cell.
26d2bead [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Zero‑width chars (combining marks, ZWJ, variation selectors) take 0 cells.
26d2bead [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2bead [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #If you mix these, cursor positioning, box drawing, and text alignment will all break.
26d2beae [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beae [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #The good news: you don’t need a perfect implementation for a prototype. A reasonable approximation
26d2beae [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #based on Unicode ranges will get you 95% of the way. Here’s a compact `charWidth` function you can
26d2beae [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #drop into your project:
26d2beaf [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beaf [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beaf [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #export function charWidth(ch: string): number {
26d2beb0 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	const code = ch.codePointAt(0)!;
26d2beb0 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb0 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	// Control characters
26d2beb0 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	if (code < 32 || (code >= 0x7F && code <= 0x9F)) return 0;
26d2beb0 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb1 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	// Combining diacritics, variation selectors, etc.
26d2beb1 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	if (
26d2beb1 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x0300 && code <= 0x036F) ||
26d2beb1 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x1AB0 && code <= 0x1AFF) ||
26d2beb2 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x1DC0 && code <= 0x1DFF) ||
26d2beb2 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x20D0 && code <= 0x20FF) ||
26d2beb2 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0xFE20 && code <= 0xFE2F)
26d2beb2 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	) return 0;
26d2beb3 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb3 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	// East Asian Wide / Fullwidth
26d2beb3 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	if (
26d2beb3 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x1100 && code <= 0x115F) || // Hangul Jamo
26d2beb3 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x2E80 && code <= 0xA4CF) || // CJK radicals etc.
26d2beb4 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0xAC00 && code <= 0xD7A3) || // Hangul syllables
26d2beb4 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0xF900 && code <= 0xFAFF) || // CJK compatibility
26d2beb4 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0xFE30 && code <= 0xFE4F) ||
26d2beb4 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0xFF00 && code <= 0xFF60) ||
26d2beb5 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0xFFE0 && code <= 0xFFE6)
26d2beb5 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	) return 2;
26d2beb5 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb5 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	// Emoji blocks
26d2beb5 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	if (
26d2beb6 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x1F300 && code <= 0x1FAFF) ||
26d2beb6 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		(code >= 0x2600 && code <= 0x27BF)
26d2beb6 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	) return 2;
26d2beb6 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb7 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	return 1;
26d2beb7 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #}
26d2beb7 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb8 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb8 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #You can then write a `stringWidth` helper that sums widths across code points:
26d2beb8 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb8 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2beb8 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #export function stringWidth(text: string): number {
26d2beb9 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	let width = 0;
26d2beb9 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	for (const ch of Array.from(text)) {
26d2beb9 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #		width += charWidth(ch);
26d2beb9 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	}
26d2beba [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #	return width;
26d2beba [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #}
26d2beba [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2bebb [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2bebb [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #• Caveats:*
26d2bebb [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d2bebb [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Grapheme clusters (e.g., an emoji with skin tone, or a family emoji made of ZWJ
26d2bebc [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #sequences) are more complex. You can either iterate by code point as above, or use
26d2bebc [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`Intl.Segmenter("en", {granularity: "grapheme"})` to handle clusters, then sum the widths. But for
26d2bebc [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #most UI work, the simple code‑point loop is sufficient.
26d2bebc [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Ambiguous width characters (like box‑drawing `━`, `╭`, etc.) are technically “East Asian
26d2bebd [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Ambiguous” – their width depends on the terminal’s locale (often 1 in Western locales, 2 in CJK
