26d35778 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.022 146.9KB 19.51s
26d35778 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Thanks for sharing the updated files. A few things stand out — and one explains precisely why
26d35779 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`GRAPHEME_DISABLE` is being ignored.
26d35779 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35779 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #1. You’re sending disable, not enable
26d35779 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35779 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #In `bootTerminal()` you wrote:
26d3577a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(GRAPHEME_DISABLE));
26d3577a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #That sends `\x1b[?2027l`. If you want the terminal to enable grapheme cluster handling, you
26d3577b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #should be sending `GRAPHEME_ENABLE` (`\x1b[?2027h`). So the current behavior is exactly what you’d
26d3577b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #expect: you’re asking the terminal to turn off the mode, and if the terminal doesn’t
26d3577c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #support it, it ignores the request.
26d3577c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #If you actually want to test whether the terminal supports mode 2027, you can try sending
26d3577c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #`GRAPHEME_ENABLE` instead. But even then, many terminals simply don’t implement this DEC private
26d3577d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #mode, and they’ll silently ignore it. That’s not a bug in your code — it’s a limitation of the
26d3577d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #terminal.
26d3577d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #2. How to determine support? DECRQM vs. CPR
26d3577e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #You already added `QUERY_2027` but never used it. That’s the DECRQM (Device Request Mode)
26d3577e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #query:
26d3577e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #\x1b[?2027$p
26d3577f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3577f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35780 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #If the terminal supports DECRQM, it will respond with something like:
26d35780 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35780 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35780 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #\x1b[?2027;0$y   // mode not recognized
26d35781 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #\x1b[?2027;1$y   // mode set
26d35781 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #\x1b[?2027;2$y   // mode reset
26d35781 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35781 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35782 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #You can read that from stdin and parse. However, DECRQM itself is not universally implemented, so
26d35782 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #absence of a response doesn’t necessarily mean mode 2027 is unsupported.
26d35782 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35782 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #The cursor position report (CPR) approach is more universal but messier:
26d35783 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35783 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Move cursor to column 1.
26d35783 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Write the test cluster `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦`.
26d35783 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Send `\x1b[6n` (DSR – request cursor position).
26d35784 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Read the response `ESC [ row ; col R`.
26d35784 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- If the cursor advanced by 2 columns, the terminal likely treated the cluster as one wide glyph.
26d35784 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #If it advanced by 8 (or something else), it did not.
26d35784 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35785 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #This works on almost any ANSI terminal, but requires asynchronous read with timeout and careful
26d35785 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #handling of partial input. It’s doable, but probably overkill for your current stage.
26d35785 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35785 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #3. A more fundamental issue: your width calc is still naive
26d35786 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35786 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #Regardless of what the terminal does, `graphemeWidth()` currently does:
26d35786 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35786 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35787 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #const codePoint = cluster.codePointAt(0)!
26d35787 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35787 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35787 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #For a cluster like `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦`, that only examines the first code point and returns 2. But if the
26d35788 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #terminal renders it as four separate emoji (each 2 cells), your `stringWidth` will be wrong —
26d35788 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #you’ll calculate 2 cells when the actual output is 8 cells. That will break your layout.
26d35788 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d35788 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #The whole point of mode 2027 is to make the terminal agree with your width calculation. If
26d35789 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #the terminal ignores the mode, your calculations must match the actual rendering. Since you
26d35789 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #can’t control the terminal, the robust approach is to use `Intl.Segmenter` to split into true
26d35789 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #grapheme clusters, then compute width based on the entire cluster (not just the first code point).
26d35789 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #We discussed this earlier; the code hasn’t been updated yet.
26d35789 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3578a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #So the next step is to replace `graphemeWidth` with a rasterizer that:
26d3578a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3578a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #1. Uses `Intl.Segmenter` to iterate clusters.
26d3578a [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #2. For each cluster, checks if it contains ZWJ (`\u200D`) or multiple wide code points, and treats
26d3578b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #it as 2 cells.
26d3578b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #3. Falls back to per‑code‑point summing otherwise.
26d3578b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3578b [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #This won’t perfectly match every terminal, but it gives you a sane baseline. If a terminal
26d3578c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #misbehaves, you can tweak one constant.
26d3578c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3578c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #4. Suggestions
26d3578c [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3578d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Change `bootTerminal()` to send `GRAPHEME_ENABLE` instead of `GRAPHEME_DISABLE` – even if
26d3578d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #ignored, it’s harmless and might help on supporting terminals.
26d3578d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Keep `QUERY_2027` for now; you can implement a DECRQM test later if you want.
26d3578d [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Upgrade `graphemeWidth` to use `Intl.Segmenter` – this is the real fix for layout correctness.
26d3578e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #- Watch out for the `native/` import path: `gadget.ts` imports from `./native/terminal.ts`, which
26d3578e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #matches your file structure, so that’s fine.
26d3578e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
26d3578e [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #If you’d like, I can provide a concrete implementation of a `graphemeWidth` using `Intl.Segmenter`
26d3578f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #that accounts for ZWJ sequences and multi‑emoji clusters — ready to drop into `terminal.ts`.
26d3578f [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] #
