26e7466d [nitro@ryzen5] is Date getHours 1 based in js?
26e7474b [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.000 493B 13.46s
26e7474b [glm-5.3-flash] #No — `getHours()` is zero-based. It returns a number from 0 to 23, where midnight is 0, noon is 12,
26e7474b [glm-5.3-flash] #and 11 PM is 23.
26e7474c [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e7474c [glm-5.3-flash] #Worth noting the same applies to `getMinutes()`, `getSeconds()`, and `getMilliseconds()`. The
26e7474c [glm-5.3-flash] #commonly confused ones are:
26e7474c [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e7474d [glm-5.3-flash] #- `getMonth()` — also zero-based (0 = January through 11 = December)
26e7474d [glm-5.3-flash] #- `getDate()` — one-based (1 to 31)
26e7474d [glm-5.3-flash] #- `getDay()` — zero-based day of week (0 = Sunday through 6 = Saturday)
26e7474d [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e7474e [glm-5.3-flash] #If you need UTC instead of local time, `getUTCHours()` follows the same 0–23 convention.
26e7474e [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f7d [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 17.80KB 102.90s
26e74f7e [glm-5.3-flash] #Reviewed all five. Here's what stood out, roughly in order of usefulness:
26e74f7e [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f7e [glm-5.3-flash] #• time.ts*
26e74f7e [glm-5.3-flash] #- The frame width math mixes character counts with display cells. `timeString()` produces exactly
26e74f7f [glm-5.3-flash] #32 characters (nice touch), but the fullwidth digits occupy 2 cells each, so the rendered line is
26e74f7f [glm-5.3-flash] #~36 columns against a 32-column box — the right border will drift or overlap. Since you already
26e74f7f [glm-5.3-flash] #have `stringWidth()` in stringwidth.ts, padding the interior lines to a target cell width
26e74f7f [glm-5.3-flash] #via that function would make `frame()` safe for arbitrary content, including the todo's color grid
26e74f80 [glm-5.3-flash] #labels later.
26e74f80 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `fullWidth` (the ASCII 32–126 table) is declared but never used — `wideNumber` covers everything
26e74f80 [glm-5.3-flash] #the clock needs.
26e74f80 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `toWideDigits()` takes one parameter but both call sites pass two; harmless, but trimming the
26e74f81 [glm-5.3-flash] #calls or widening the signature would keep intent clear. Similarly `stringDigits(num, tenGlyphs)`
26e74f81 [glm-5.3-flash] #assumes `num < 100` and the name suggests tens-glyphs rather than digit-glyphs.
26e74f81 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f81 [glm-5.3-flash] #• stringwidth.ts*
26e74f82 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Line 4 has a stray statement, `Intl.localesargument`, that looks like leftover scratch — it
26e74f82 [glm-5.3-flash] #evaluates to undefined so it won't throw, but it shouldn't ship.
26e74f82 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Flags like 🇺🇸 pass through your ranges by accident: regional indicators (U+1F1E6–1F1FF) sit
26e74f82 [glm-5.3-flash] #below your 0x1F300 emoji floor, so they score as narrow singles and sum to 2. Correct
26e74f83 [glm-5.3-flash] #answer, fragile reasoning — an explicit range or comment would prevent a future refactor from
26e74f83 [glm-5.3-flash] #breaking it.
26e74f83 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Coverage gaps versus EastAsianWidth: CJK Extensions B onward (U+20000+) are Wide and currently
26e74f83 [glm-5.3-flash] #width 1; U+3099–309A (combining katakana voicing marks) should be zero-width but land as 1. Your
26e74f84 [glm-5.3-flash] #blanket 2600–27BF generously covers most emoji-presentation symbols but also sweeps in some narrow
26e74f84 [glm-5.3-flash] #ones (scissors, ✔). Given the README's F/W/H/Na/N/A research, the "A" (Ambiguous) class is the one
26e74f84 [glm-5.3-flash] #your implementation can't express yet — a boolean "treat ambiguous as wide" toggle is the usual
26e74f84 [glm-5.3-flash] #convention for CJK-targeted terminals.
26e74f85 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Mode 2027 note: mazgui sends `GRAPHEME_DISABLE` while the whole architecture segments
26e74f85 [glm-5.3-flash] #client-side. Once rendering stabilizes, enabling 2027 is what convinces the terminal itself to
26e74f85 [glm-5.3-flash] #treat 👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 as one cell group — likely where you want to end up.
26e74f86 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f86 [glm-5.3-flash] #• language.ts*
26e74f86 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Inside the timeZone branch you call `Intl.supportedValuesOf("timeZone")` a second time; `values`
26e74f86 [glm-5.3-flash] #already holds it.
26e74f87 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Slicing `toLocaleString` output for " GMT" works, but `Intl.DateTimeFormat("en", { timeZone,
26e74f87 [glm-5.3-flash] #timeZoneName }).format(now)` returns the offset alone with no date portion, which drops the indexOf
26e74f87 [glm-5.3-flash] #dance entirely. Your `"+"+0` fallback for plain "GMT" is still needed either way.
26e74f87 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f88 [glm-5.3-flash] #• mazgui.ts*
26e74f88 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `updateConsole(panx,pany)` parameters are untyped — `deno check` will flag implicit any under
26e74f88 [glm-5.3-flash] #strict settings.
26e74f88 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `class panel` and the `writeConsole` import are currently unused; `oldKeys` is assigned but only
26e74f89 [glm-5.3-flash] #`oldStatus` participates in change detection.
26e74f89 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `borderStyle` is duplicated verbatim in time.ts — a shared module would prevent the styles
26e74f89 [glm-5.3-flash] #diverging as gadgets multiply.
26e74f89 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f8a [glm-5.3-flash] #• relay.md is a single "# gadget" heading — presumably the spec slot for the
26e74f8a [glm-5.3-flash] #GADGET_ constants living in mazgui.ts.
26e74f8a [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e74f8a [glm-5.3-flash] #The overall direction (client-side segmentation + manual width tables instead of trusting terminal
26e74f8b [glm-5.3-flash] #wcwidth) is sound for a desktop manager, since you control presentation end-to-end. Happy to draft
26e74f8b [glm-5.3-flash] #the shared width/padding module or fill in relay.md if you want either next.
26e74f8b [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e75526 [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 24.46KB 13.49s
26e75526 [glm-5.3-flash] #Diffed against the previous version — one change: both `toWideDigits()` call sites now drop the
26e75526 [glm-5.3-flash] #stray second argument, which resolves the signature mismatch flagged earlier (and accounts for
26e75526 [glm-5.3-flash] #exactly the 4-byte size delta).
26e75527 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e75527 [glm-5.3-flash] #Still open from last time:
26e75527 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e75527 [glm-5.3-flash] #- The unused `fullWidth` table.
26e75527 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `borderStyle` duplicated with mazgui.ts.
26e75528 [glm-5.3-flash] #- The core layout issue: `frame(32, ...)` sizes borders by character count while the clock line's
26e75528 [glm-5.3-flash] #fullwidth digits render at 2 cells each, so the interior is wider than the box. Wiring
26e75528 [glm-5.3-flash] #`stringWidth()` into an interior-padding helper inside `frame()` would close that gap permanently
26e75528 [glm-5.3-flash] #rather than tuning the magic "32".
26e75529 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e75529 [glm-5.3-flash] #The rest of the file is byte-for-byte unchanged as far as I can tell.
26e75529 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e756d9 [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 30.81KB 18.04s
26e756d9 [glm-5.3-flash] #Diffed against the last version — the stray `Intl.localesargument` scratch statement is gone, which
26e756d9 [glm-5.3-flash] #fully accounts for the 26-byte reduction. Everything else is unchanged.
26e756da [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e756da [glm-5.3-flash] #Still carrying over from the earlier review, since none of these moved:
26e756da [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e756da [glm-5.3-flash] #- Regional indicators (🇺🇸) get the right answer only by coincidence of your range floors
26e756db [glm-5.3-flash] #— worth an explicit comment or range before a refactor breaks it silently.
26e756db [glm-5.3-flash] #- CJK Extension B+ (U+20000 and up, e.g. 𠀀) still scores width 1 but renders wide.
26e756db [glm-5.3-flash] #- U+3099–309A (combining katakana voicing marks) land as width 1 instead of 0.
26e756db [glm-5.3-flash] #- No representation for EastAsianWidth "Ambiguous" yet — relevant once mazgui targets CJK-locale
26e756dc [glm-5.3-flash] #terminals.
26e756dc [glm-5.3-flash] #- Roughly half the file is commented-out dead code (the old `graphemeWidth`/`stringWidth` pair and
26e756dc [glm-5.3-flash] #the cursor helpers). Fine while iterating, but it will start obscuring the live implementation as
26e756dc [glm-5.3-flash] #this module becomes the shared foundation for time.ts padding and the gadget layout.
26e756dd [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e756dd [glm-5.3-flash] #The active implementation itself looks solid for its current scope — ZWJ handling,
26e756dd [glm-5.3-flash] #multi-wide-cluster collapsing, and zero-width ranges all behave sensibly for the clock and title
26e756dd [glm-5.3-flash] #strings you're feeding through them.
26e756de [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e79a24 [nitro@ryzen5] what year was r3000 processor released?
26e79ac8 [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 32.25KB 8.67s
26e79ac8 [glm-5.3-flash] #The MIPS R3000 was released in 1988 by MIPS Computer Systems. It was a 32-bit RISC design
26e79ac8 [glm-5.3-flash] #implementing the MIPS I instruction set, following the earlier R2000.
26e79ac9 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e79ac9 [glm-5.3-flash] #It saw wide adoption in late-80s/early-90s workstations — Silicon Graphics and DEC both built
26e79ac9 [glm-5.3-flash] #machines around it — and its R3000A variant is probably best remembered as the main CPU inside the
26e79ac9 [glm-5.3-flash] #original Sony PlayStation in 1994.
26e79aca [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e79ee7 [nitro@ryzen5] and the motorola 68000?
26e7a028 [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 32.85KB 9.14s
26e7a028 [glm-5.3-flash] #The Motorola 68000 was released in 1979 (with systems using it shipping from 1980 onward).
26e7a028 [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e7a029 [glm-5.3-flash] #It occupied an interesting middle ground: a 32-bit architecture internally — 32-bit registers and
26e7a029 [glm-5.3-flash] #flat address space design — but with a 16-bit external data bus and 24-bit addressing, which is why
26e7a029 [glm-5.3-flash] #it's often described as 16/32-bit. It drove roughly 1 million transistors... actually closer to
26e7a029 [glm-5.3-flash] #68,000, one of the largest chips of its era, built on an HMOS process running at 4–8 MHz initially.
26e7a02a [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e7a02a [glm-5.3-flash] #Its adoption list is legendary: the original Apple Macintosh, Commodore Amiga, Atari ST, Sega Mega
26e7a02a [glm-5.3-flash] #Drive/Genesis, and countless Sun, HP, and Silicon Graphics workstations — making it the dominant
26e7a02a [glm-5.3-flash] #CPU of late-80s personal computing before Intel's x86 took the mainstream.
26e7a02b [glm-5.3-flash] #
26e7a02b [glm-5.3-flash] #Fun contrast with your earlier question: the 68000 predates the R3000 by about nine years, and MIPS
26e7a02b [glm-5.3-flash] #machines like the SGI Indigo eventually displaced 68k-based workstations in the early 90s.
26e7a02b [glm-5.3-flash] #
