27026651 [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 53.33KB 24.35s
27026651 [glm-5.3-flash] #`uint` isn't a standard C/C++ type — it's a BSD/POSIX typedef for `unsigned int`, defined in
27026651 [glm-5.3-flash] #`<sys/types.h>` (which newlib provides on the Pico). The SDK headers use it everywhere and pull it
27026651 [glm-5.3-flash] #in transitively, so once `hardware/gpio.h` is included it's available.
27026651 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026652 [glm-5.3-flash] #Two things to watch in `picomain.cpp` specifically:
27026652 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026652 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Your `gpio` declaration sits at the very top of the file, before `#include
27026653 [glm-5.3-flash] #<hardware/pio.h>` and friends. Using `uint` there needs an explicit `#include <sys/types.h>` at the
27026653 [glm-5.3-flash] #top, or the declaration moved below the SDK includes.
27026653 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `uint` won't exist on host builds or MSVC, so if this file ever compiles off-device, `unsigned
27026653 [glm-5.3-flash] #int` is the safer spelling.
27026653 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026654 [glm-5.3-flash] #Simplest option that avoids all of that: keep the array of plain literals —
27026654 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026654 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026654 [glm-5.3-flash] #const int gpio[]={0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,14,15,16,17,18,19,20,21,22,26,27};
27026655 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026655 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27026655 [glm-5.3-flash] #`int` converts to the SDK's `uint` parameters implicitly with no warnings for these values, and no
27026656 [glm-5.3-flash] #extra include is needed.
27026656 [glm-5.3-flash] #
