2702a8e8 [glm-5.3-flash] #
2702a8e8 [glm-5.3-flash] #uint32_t readGpioGroup(const uint *pins, uint pinCount){
2702a8e9 [glm-5.3-flash] #	uint32_t all=gpio_get_all();
2702a8e9 [glm-5.3-flash] #	uint32_t result=0;
2702a8e9 [glm-5.3-flash] #	for(uint i=0;i<pinCount;i++){
2702a8e9 [glm-5.3-flash] #		if(all & (1u<<pins[i])) result|=(1u<<i);
2702a8ea [glm-5.3-flash] #	}
2702a8ea [glm-5.3-flash] #	return result;
2702a8ea [glm-5.3-flash] #}
2702a8ea [glm-5.3-flash] #
2702a8eb [glm-5.3-flash] #
2702a8eb [glm-5.3-flash] #Two things to note for your wiring:
2702a8eb [glm-5.3-flash] #
2702a8ec [glm-5.3-flash] #- Your buttons are active-low (pull-ups, pressed = 0), and your current `fire`/`keys` code treats a
2702a8ec [glm-5.3-flash] #high as set — so a one-shot `uint32_t pressed = ~gpio_get_all();` followed by bit tests would fix
2702a8ec [glm-5.3-flash] #the inversion and collapse both reads into a single register access.
2702a8ec [glm-5.3-flash] #- The output side has the same family: `gpio_put_masked(mask, value)`, `gpio_set_mask()`,
2702a8ed [glm-5.3-flash] #`gpio_clr_mask()`, `gpio_set_dir_masked()` — handy for the LED/palette-style writes.
2702a8ed [glm-5.3-flash] #
2702a8ed [glm-5.3-flash] #On RP2350, pins 32–47 live in a second register (`gpio_in_hi`), but `gpio_get_all()` handles both
2702a8ed [glm-5.3-flash] #banks for you, so there's no reason to touch the registers directly.
