2702cc5d [glm-5.3-flash] #   `gpio_init()` resets a pin to SIO function, so if `stdio_init_all()` did set up UART on
2702cc5e [glm-5.3-flash] #0/1, this loop would strip the UART function off them again. Any subsequent `log()`/`printf` output
2702cc5e [glm-5.3-flash] #via UART would go nowhere after that point.
2702cc5e [glm-5.3-flash] #
2702cc5e [glm-5.3-flash] #So the practical answer: if UART stdio is disabled in CMake, nothing sets UART pins and you're
2702cc5f [glm-5.3-flash] #clean. If it's enabled, pins 0 and 1 get configured by `stdio_init_all()` and then immediately
2702cc5f [glm-5.3-flash] #clobbered by the ROM input init — so you'd want to drop them from that vector or re-apply the UART
2702cc5f [glm-5.3-flash] #function afterwards.
2702cc5f [glm-5.3-flash] #
