270321ec [glm-5.3-flash] #picomain.cpp has exactly one change since the last share: `READ_TOUCH` is now enabled (the −2 chars
270321ec [glm-5.3-flash] #in the file length is the removed `//`). That's worth flagging first, because it cuts across the
270321ec [glm-5.3-flash] #1.14 downgrade:
270321ec [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321ed [glm-5.3-flash] #- The 1.14 has no touch controller, so there's nothing on the end of the reads.
270321ed [glm-5.3-flash] #- TP_CS/TP_IRQ (GP16/17) sit inside the `USE_ROM` vector, and `initTouch()` runs before the
270321ed [glm-5.3-flash] #ROM init loop in `picoInstance()` — so the ROM loop resets CS back to a plain input and touch is
270321ed [glm-5.3-flash] #dead even if a chip were attached.
270321ed [glm-5.3-flash] #- `Touch_Read_ADC()` drops the shared SPI bus to `TOUCH_SPI_BAUD` and back on every poll — wasted
270321ee [glm-5.3-flash] #time each frame reading nothing.
270321ee [glm-5.3-flash] #- The touch readout bars draw at y=160/180, past the 135px panel, so they'd be invisible
270321ee [glm-5.3-flash] #regardless.
270321ee [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321ee [glm-5.3-flash] #Cleanest fix: tie `READ_TOUCH` to the board selection — define it only under
270321ef [glm-5.3-flash] #`WAVESHARE_TOUCHLCD_35`, or move it to CMake next to the board macro so it can't drift out of sync
270321ef [glm-5.3-flash] #again.
270321ef [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321ef [glm-5.3-flash] #• picotool.h pin map vs the ROM vector:*
270321f0 [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321f0 [glm-5.3-flash] #╭──────────┬───────────────────────────────┬──────────────────╮
270321f0 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  GPIO    │  Claimed by                   │  In ROM vector?  │
270321f1 [glm-5.3-flash] #├──────────┼───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────┤
270321f1 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  0–1     │  UART_TX/RX (defines only)    │  yes             │
270321f1 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  2–3     │  KEY2/KEY3, FIRE (=2)         │  yes             │
270321f1 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  4–5     │  SPK_R/L                      │  yes             │
270321f1 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  6–7     │  EPD_SDA/SCL (unused by LCD)  │  yes             │
270321f2 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  8–13    │  LCD DC/CS/CLK/MOSI/RST/BL    │  **no**          │
270321f2 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  15, 17  │  KEY0/KEY1                    │  yes             │
270321f2 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  16–17   │  TP_CS/TP_IRQ                 │  yes             │
270321f2 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  18–22   │  SD (dormant)                 │  yes             │
270321f3 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  25      │  POWER_LED                    │  no              │
270321f3 [glm-5.3-flash] #│  26–27   │  ADC0/1                       │  yes             │
270321f3 [glm-5.3-flash] #╰──────────┴───────────────────────────────┴──────────────────╯
270321f3 [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321f4 [glm-5.3-flash] #The good news: the EPD_* block is the real LCD — DC 8, CS 9, CLK 10, MOSI 11, RST 12, BL 13 — which
270321f4 [glm-5.3-flash] #is exactly the Waveshare Pico-LCD-1.14 add-on pinout, and all of 8–13 fall in the ROM vector's gap.
270321f4 [glm-5.3-flash] #Display vs ROM is finally conflict-free. GP10/11 are valid SPI1 SCK/MOSI, and MISO staying
270321f4 [glm-5.3-flash] #commented is correct for a write-only ST7789.
270321f5 [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321f5 [glm-5.3-flash] #Other observations on picotool.h:
270321f5 [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321f5 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `WAVESHARE_LCD_114` currently only changes `EPD_RST_PIN` (12 vs 15). The 240×135
270321f6 [glm-5.3-flash] #dimensions come from the `#else` of the `LCD_2_8_touch` test, so a build with none of the three
270321f6 [glm-5.3-flash] #board macros defined silently gets 1.14 dimensions with RST on 15. Keying the dimensions off the
270321f6 [glm-5.3-flash] #board macros too (the `#if defined(...) || defined(...)` form) would make the selection complete in
270321f6 [glm-5.3-flash] #one place.
270321f7 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `I2C_PORT` is defined as `spi1` — copy-paste leftover. Anything that ever uses it will
270321f7 [glm-5.3-flash] #misbehave; should be an i2c instance or deleted.
270321f7 [glm-5.3-flash] #- Duplicate names: `FIRE_PIN 2` == `KEY2_PIN 2`, and `SIO_CS_PIN 9` == `EPD_CS_PIN 9`. Also
270321f7 [glm-5.3-flash] #`SD_CS_PIN 22` is defined here and in picomain.cpp — identical value so the redefinition is
270321f8 [glm-5.3-flash] #legal, but one home would be better.
270321f8 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `PicoButton` leaves `prev`/`state` uninitialised. Your globals `a`/`b`/`c` are
270321f8 [glm-5.3-flash] #zero-initialised so it works today, but a stack-allocated one would report a phantom press on the
270321f8 [glm-5.3-flash] #first `set()`.
270321f8 [glm-5.3-flash] #- `Bitmap` at full screen is ~63KB of heap (240×135×2) — a quarter of RP2040 SRAM, worth
270321f9 [glm-5.3-flash] #remembering if you ever hold more than one. Your own TODO on `rectangle()`'s x/w overflow stands,
270321f9 [glm-5.3-flash] #and `plot()` is likewise unchecked.
270321f9 [glm-5.3-flash] #- UART_TX/RX on 0/1 are defines only — nothing applies them, consistent with what we found
270321f9 [glm-5.3-flash] #earlier, and both pins are in the ROM vector anyway.
270321f9 [glm-5.3-flash] #
270321f9 [glm-5.3-flash] #Main action item from this pass: gate `READ_TOUCH` behind the touch board, since everything else on
270321fa [glm-5.3-flash] #the 1.14 path lines up cleanly.
270321fa [glm-5.3-flash] #
27034c4f [nitro@ryzen5] how do i clear pullup
27035053 [glm-5.3-flash] #🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.002 68.13KB 63.38s
27035053 [glm-5.3-flash] #Two ways, both in `hardware/gpio.h`:
27035053 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035054 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035054 [glm-5.3-flash] #gpio_set_pulls(pin, false, false);	// explicit: clears both up and down
27035054 [glm-5.3-flash] #gpio_disable_pulls(pin);		// same thing, inline wrapper in newer SDKs
27035054 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035055 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035055 [glm-5.3-flash] #Two gotchas worth knowing:
27035055 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035055 [glm-5.3-flash] #1. `gpio_pull_down(pin)` is not "clear" — it swaps pull-up for pull-down. And RP2040 pads
27035056 [glm-5.3-flash] #come out of chip reset with pull-downs enabled, so to get a genuinely floating input you
27035056 [glm-5.3-flash] #need to clear both, which is what the calls above do.
27035056 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035057 [glm-5.3-flash] #2. `gpio_init()` and `gpio_set_function()` don't touch pulls. They only change the function
27035057 [glm-5.3-flash] #select in the IO bank; the pad settings (pulls, drive strength, hysteresis) persist. So a pull-up
27035057 [glm-5.3-flash] #set once stays set across re-init.
27035057 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035058 [glm-5.3-flash] #That second point matters for your code: `initTouch()` sets a pull-up on TP_IRQ (GP17), and the
27035058 [glm-5.3-flash] #`USE_ROM` loop later does `gpio_init(17)` — the pull-up survives that. If you want the ROM pins
27035058 [glm-5.3-flash] #truly floating, the loop needs to clear them explicitly:
27035058 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035059 [glm-5.3-flash] #
27035059 [glm-5.3-flash] #for(size_t i=0;i<gpio.size();i++){
27035059 [glm-5.3-flash] #	uint pin=gpio[i];
27035059 [glm-5.3-flash] #	gpio_init(pin);
2703505a [glm-5.3-flash] #	gpio_set_dir(pin,GPIO_IN);
2703505a [glm-5.3-flash] #	gpio_set_pulls(pin,false,false);
2703505a [glm-5.3-flash] #}
2703505b [glm-5.3-flash] #
2703505b [glm-5.3-flash] #
2703505b [glm-5.3-flash] #Conversely, the same persistence is what makes your button init work — `gpio_pull_up()` in the
2703505b [glm-5.3-flash] #`#else` branch doesn't need re-applying each loop.
2703505b [glm-5.3-flash] #
